WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.880
OK, so we'll get started.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.490
Welcome to lecture 15.

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:14.730
We've got two pieces
left to the class.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.700
And that's going to be various
aspects of post-training.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.680
Up until now, we've
focused very much

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.160
on the big pre-training
systems data components.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.660
And then now, we're going to
take the big pre-trained model,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.660
and we're going to make it
useful and safe in various ways.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.110
So that's going to be the
next two lectures from me.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:37.980
Today is going to be RLHF,
and safety alignment stuff.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:44.160
And then Thursday is going to
be RL from verifiable rewards.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.030
So things like reasoning
training and math, and so on

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:49.170
will be on Thursday.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.310
As I said before, today we're
going to shift from pre-training

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.510
to post-training.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.970
Percy in the very last
lecture did cover some stuff

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.010
about post-training data.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.380
But really, I think,
the focus today

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.740
is going to be in going from
essentially this big transition

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.120
that we saw in the field.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.460
So we have GPT3, really
remarkable system,

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.000
really impressive, lots of
pre-training, lots of compute.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.240
But this is not really
a useful system.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.650
I guess there was a couple
startups around building ad copy

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:19.530
and things like that.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.700
But it was not very useful.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.950
It didn't follow instructions.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.530
It didn't do anything
particularly too

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:27.090
interesting from a
product point of view.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.320
And then all of a
sudden, we got ChatGPT.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.510
And ChatGPT can do all
sorts of amazing things

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:33.510
and follow instructions.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.352
And we've kind of seen what that
has done to society since then.

00:01:37.352 --> 00:01:40.380
So today's focus is going to
be on this arrow right here.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.200
How do we take a pre-trained
system like GPT3?

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.300
And how do we make
something like ChatGPT?

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.900
And then we're going to
try to get to the nuts

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.750
and bolts of that process.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:54.980
And I think most
of you have never

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.050
worked on things like
controllable generation

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.350
or the previous generation
of text generation systems,

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.260
but really like
modern instruction

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.600
following models
are just amazing.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.520
This is one of my
favorite examples

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:12.020
from Sebastien Bubeck's
Sparks of AGI paper in 2023,

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.200
around when GPT4 came out.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.390
But it can follow this very
long block of nested compound

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:20.660
instructions.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.740
And then, combine that with
its coding capability to output

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.740
you zero shot matplotlib code.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.990
And I think all of you just
take this for granted now.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.407
It's like, yes,
of course, ChatGPT

00:02:30.407 --> 00:02:32.500
can follow 10
instructions at once.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.760
But it's just amazing
that it can do this.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.360
And I think part of my
excitement about this lecture

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.107
is the fact that it
can do all of this.

00:02:40.107 --> 00:02:42.190
And the other thing that
I think is very important

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:43.660
is that now that
these systems are

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.870
out in the wild, safety
and content moderation just

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.160
becomes really important.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.530
Safety from the perspective of
these models might get misused,

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.770
someone might try to
use them for scams.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.450
And also content
moderation if you're

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.250
thinking about this being useful
products that you can ship

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.570
and people will pay for.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.820
People don't really
want to pay for

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.990
or put ads on systems that
are horrifically toxic.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.600
I think one of the big,
big reasons why ChatGPT

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.820
has been so
successful is that it

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.390
has really significant
guardrails around it.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.140
Given that, the
goal today is to try

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.970
to enable much tighter, better
controls on language models.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:27.090
Pre-training, you can
think of the mental model

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.850
can be that it packs the model
with all sorts of capabilities.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.840
After pre-training, the
model is able to-- somewhere

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.110
within the parameters-- do lots
of things like reason and answer

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.430
questions.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.778
But it's not going to
do them out of the box.

00:03:40.778 --> 00:03:42.570
And so today, what
we're going to try to do

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.000
is get models to do
that out of the box.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.010
And so what we're going
to do is to collect

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.080
data of various
kinds of behaviors

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.120
that we do want from
the language model

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.787
and train it to do those things.

00:03:52.787 --> 00:03:54.870
And so the questions that
you should be asking now

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.920
is, what does that
data look like?

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.655
How hard is it to
collect that data?

00:03:58.655 --> 00:04:00.280
Percy has touched on
this a little bit.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.980
But given the
importance of the data,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.730
I'm going to re-emphasize
it a little bit.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.430
I'm going to have
some interactive--

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.665
some exercises to go over that.

00:04:08.665 --> 00:04:10.290
And then there's
algorithmic questions.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.650
How do we make use of that data?

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.305
Certain kinds of
data are easy to use.

00:04:15.305 --> 00:04:17.040
If you have expert
demonstrations,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.550
you just train to imitate that.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.467
But if you have things
like pairwise feedback,

00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:22.560
like model output A is
better than model output

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.430
B, how do we make use of that?

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.260
And then finally, how
do we scale this up?

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.110
How do we do the usual
things that we've

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:29.700
been doing in this class?

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.300
So the structure of
this lecture is roughly

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.410
going to mirror the
InstructGPT paper,

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:38.010
because a lot of the
post-training pipeline that we

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:42.060
have today is still off
the InstructGPT paper.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.180
And so the first
part of this lecture

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.710
is going to be on
supervised fine-tuning.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.680
So if you look at the
InstructGPT paper,

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.420
you'll see this diagram that
roughly describes a three step

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.140
process for building an
instructional following model.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.783
So part one of this lecture is
going to be the leftmost part.

00:04:58.783 --> 00:05:00.700
The part where what we're
going to do is we're

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.410
going to do
supervised fine-tuning

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:03.740
on expert demonstration.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:05.980
And then part two, we're
going to follow the next two

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:07.355
parts of this structure.

00:05:07.355 --> 00:05:08.980
We're going to talk
about reinforcement

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.190
learning and pairwise feedback.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:16.930
Broadly, I'm going to say
that the ingredients in order

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.660
to get this first part working,
I mean, there's two things

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.610
that we have to think about.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.010
The first part is
the training data.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.390
If you're going to imitate
expert demonstrations,

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.100
you better have
expert demonstrations.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.040
So what does that look like?

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.540
And then the second thing I want
to talk about is the method.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:37.090
You have data now, how are
you going to adapt to it?

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:38.570
And there's an obvious answer.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:39.920
I'll talk about this again.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.710
But just do gradient descent.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:45.080
But there's also a kind of
non-obvious part to this answer.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.350
And in case you
haven't been following

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:48.980
how people build
these models today,

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.700
this might be still surprising.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:54.760
So I'll leave that as
a teaser for later.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.880
OK, so in Percy's
lecture, he's mentioned

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.530
already several different
kinds of instruction data.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.930
But today, we're going to
walk through a couple of them,

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:05.513
and we're going to do a little
bit of an interactive exercise.

00:06:05.513 --> 00:06:07.480
So those of you who
have your laptops open

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.310
can use those for good.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.890
So I want to talk about
two different details.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:14.600
One of them is what's
inside these data sets.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.730
People often say
data matters a lot.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.750
I think post-training is one
place where this is even more

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:23.710
true than before, because you're
using very small amounts of data

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.565
to get exactly the
behaviors you want.

00:06:25.565 --> 00:06:27.440
So if you have noisy
instruction tuning data,

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:28.960
you're going to get some
pretty crazy behavior out

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:29.615
of your models.

00:06:29.615 --> 00:06:31.240
And then what kinds
of things should we

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:32.210
be paying attention to?

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.453
If you're in charge of
post-training data collection,

00:06:34.453 --> 00:06:35.870
what kinds of
things might matter?

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:39.610
So I have taken three
different data sets

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:43.070
from basically constructed
in three very different ways.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:46.840
You might even call them
three different paradigms

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.415
to building instruction
following or post-training data.

00:06:50.415 --> 00:06:52.040
And we're going to
go through each one.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.360
And then, we'll look
at them closely,

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:54.790
and then we'll think a
little bit about what's

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.180
going on with these data sets.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.930
So I'm going to talk about FLAN.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:01.790
This is by a bunch
of Google folks.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:06.250
And FLAN is going to be
essentially constructed

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:09.280
by aggregating a bunch
of training data sets

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.850
from NLP tasks.

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:14.290
So if you look at it, you see
all sorts of different tasks

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:16.450
like natural
instructions, V2, which

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.680
has a bunch of question
answering and things,

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:24.020
it's got T0-SF, adversarial
QA, and topic classification.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:26.110
So basically, this
was constructed

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.930
by taking existing
NLP data sets that

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.780
do all sorts of individual
tasks and then aggregating them

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.400
into one big metadata set.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.550
So this is one approach to
building such data sets.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.430
We've got OpenAssistant
on the right.

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:44.320
And this was, I think, a
pretty unique endeavour,

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.520
in which a bunch of online
enthusiasts got together

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.400
and decided to write
instruction-tuning data

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:51.550
for language models.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.440
Right after the
release of ChatGPT,

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:56.992
I think the excitement for this
kind of thing was really high.

00:07:56.992 --> 00:07:59.700
And so there's actually a lot of
good, high quality human-written

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:01.620
data from that effort.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.430
Then lastly, of course, this
is a bit of self-advertisement

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:04.930
here.

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:08.550
But as a representative
of language model

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.112
generated post-training data
or AI feedback style data,

00:08:11.112 --> 00:08:13.320
I'm going to talk a little
bit about some of the data

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.510
from Stanford alpaca.

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.665
So let's just look at examples.

00:08:17.665 --> 00:08:19.290
I think looking at
examples and talking

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.840
about them are very useful.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.880
Now, this is from
random examples

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:26.010
taken from the FLAN data set.

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:30.480
And you can see the types
of stuff that are in here.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.049
So you've got things that look
pretty normal instruction tuning

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.059
data like right highlights
for this article,

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:39.172
sauntering down leafy avenues
past Dutch step gabled

00:08:39.172 --> 00:08:40.630
buildings, dot,
dot, dot, dot, dot.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:43.230
And then in the end, there's
even more information

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:46.530
on travel in the Netherlands
at www.holland.com.

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:50.040
And then it answers the least
known of the Dutch cities,

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.280
the Hague was a village,
dot, dot, dot, dot, dot.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.270
And then it summarizes
this as a highlight.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:59.720
You've got something
like this, where this is

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.320
like, what is this text about?

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.640
Here are your four options.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.610
It's business.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:09.810
So this is a multiple choice
training thing that's happening.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.550
Percy talked about
the Enron data set.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.200
So you all can
smile a little bit.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.400
But things like this of taking
let's say maybe the Enron email

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.362
data set and you paste
back, write a subject

00:09:18.362 --> 00:09:19.820
line for this email,
and now you've

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.460
got supervision for that task.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:25.400
This one, I guess no
one here has probably

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:26.930
worked on text
generation, but this

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.270
is from a data set called E2E
where you have a database entry,

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.660
and then you're supposed
to write a sentence that

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:35.300
describes that restaurant.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:37.640
So immediately you see--

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.280
you can probably get a lot
of data for free this way.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.160
There's a lot of NLP
training data sets,

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.270
and you can put
them all together

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:47.560
and you will get a really
big aggregated data set.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.260
And so in that sense,
FLAN was ahead of its time

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:52.780
and produced a ton of data
for this kind of thing.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:54.900
But also, we see in
many ways that this

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.540
can be somewhat unnatural.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.120
We've already seen that this
Enron data set is a little bit

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:00.700
weird.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:04.210
We can really definitely see
things like, oh, here's a text.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:08.410
And then now you append the
options to turn it into a task.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:10.080
And so you can see
the surgery that you

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:15.115
have to do very visibly in order
to make this kind of data set.

00:10:15.115 --> 00:10:16.740
And I think if you
look at this, you'll

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.390
agree with me that this isn't
your usual chat interaction

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:23.070
for something like ChatGPT.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:27.400
Another example
for this is alpaca.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:31.800
This was like a really,
really early attempt

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.380
at using language models to
generate instruction tuning

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:35.400
data.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.760
And so just to describe
the procedure here,

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.540
language model was used.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:42.880
There was a seed set of
human written instructions,

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.910
and then a language model was
used to essentially generate

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:45.760
more instructions.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.430
So that's the left
column, and then you

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:50.520
use something like InstructGPT
to essentially fill

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:51.490
in the response.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:54.060
And so here, now
we have something

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.240
that looks a little bit
more like on the left,

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.400
standard ChatGPT inputs.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.970
If you compare this to
something on the left,

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:06.730
this is a very
benchmark-centric set of tasks.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:09.240
This feels a lot more
like a set of interactions

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.580
that someone might just
throw into a chatbot.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.610
And the response is
almost always in long form

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:17.290
natural language
versus with FLAN,

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:19.980
often it can be quite short,
like one word or a phrase

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:21.932
or something like that.

00:11:21.932 --> 00:11:23.320
So we see that.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.780
Of course, we also
see that on the left.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:28.120
These are, in some ways,
not very diverse inputs.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.660
They're very short instructions.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:35.160
And then OpenAssistant is the
third leg of this instruction

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.780
tuning saga.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.880
You see more complex
queries on the left.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:43.260
And then because back then, I
guess people were just really

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:46.990
into writing long, detailed
supervision for models,

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:49.510
you see actually really
detailed responses.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:53.670
And this one even has
a citation on what

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.311
makes this answer correct.

00:11:56.311 --> 00:12:02.520
And so very high quality but
also very difficult. And so now,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.610
this is the first interactive
task in this class.

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:09.600
But especially those of you that
have your laptops open, please

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:10.420
go to this URL.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.420
This should be a Google form.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:16.530
And there will be a one prompt.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:17.970
And now, we're
collectively going

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:22.330
to crowdsource a
instruction tuning response.

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:26.340
And I'll give you all, let's
say five minutes to do so.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.330
Let me know if
the link is wrong.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:30.040
But I did test this last night.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.990
So hopefully, this is working.

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:35.430
And then we'll look at
the responses briefly.

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:38.710
And then, I want to talk
about why I did this exercise.

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:42.280
There is a teachable moment here
rather than getting you just

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:43.740
off your laptops for a moment.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:49.990
OK, excellent.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.490
I think there's a decent
number of responses.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:56.980
So I'm going to
maybe put them up.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.660
Let's see if I can put them up.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:01.388
Yeah, there we go.

00:13:01.388 --> 00:13:04.630
So in many ways, I
think this reflects

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:06.328
the kinds of data you get.

00:13:06.328 --> 00:13:07.870
I mean, if anything,
you guys are all

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:10.720
motivated to do this
task than, I think,

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:12.760
the standard crowd worker.

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:13.910
But you've got the person.

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:16.310
I mean, I'm not sure
what's going on here.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.890
But this is probably ChatGPT.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.260
There's a lot of emojis.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:22.060
I'm getting trolled,
but this is--

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:24.970
I mean, I am
preparing for liftoff.

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.700
That's a good response.

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:31.040
You've got the "nahhhh
fam," which, of course,

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:34.240
is the kinds of things that
you'll get out of crowdsourcing.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:38.110
And I think hopefully, one
thing that you've seen or felt

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:41.260
as you were doing this is that
it's actually really difficult

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:43.420
to write long form responses.

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:46.762
Especially for something
that you aren't prepared for.

00:13:46.762 --> 00:13:49.720
And so you get a lot of
short responses like this.

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:51.220
It's very difficult,
I think, to get

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:54.940
people to write long, detailed
responses like this one

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:56.350
at the very top.

00:13:56.350 --> 00:14:02.030
Often, those kinds of
things are from ChatGPT.

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:04.477
And of course, you'll get
things like, I saw this one.

00:14:04.477 --> 00:14:06.310
Ice cream is a frozen
dessert typically made

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:07.140
from milk or cream.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.640
And you're going to have to
filter out those kinds of things

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.550
that you're going to get
through crowdsourcing.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:19.400
So why did I talk about this?

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:22.820
Well, of course, now you have a
sense of what this task is like.

00:14:22.820 --> 00:14:24.920
Annotators in the wild,
even if they're experts,

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:27.190
will be under time constraints.

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:30.100
And I think one
of the reasons why

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:34.420
things like AI feedback or using
LMs to try to refine or generate

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:37.210
these kinds of data has
really gotten popular

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:40.780
is if you look at the
GPT-4o response to this,

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:42.680
it's pretty good.

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:44.840
It's a pretty good
response to this question.

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:45.620
It's very long.

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:47.080
It's very detailed.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:50.350
And generating this
kind of a human response

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:53.360
is going to take a lot of
effort and a lot of cost.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:55.998
And so you have to-- if
you're in charge of human data

00:14:55.998 --> 00:14:58.540
collection at one of these labs,
you have to think about, OK,

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.030
how do we take what I showed
you in the spreadsheet

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:02.950
and incentivize people
to generate something

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.250
that looks like this instead?

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:06.260
That is no easy task at all.

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:09.505
That is a very difficult
crowdsourcing task.

00:15:09.505 --> 00:15:13.100
And so these things
that we've just seen,

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.840
they vary quite a bit
in things like length.

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.690
We saw the ChatGPT
which is bullet points,

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.030
like lots of style variations.

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:25.180
We saw in the
OpenAssistant example

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:27.820
that sometimes people
put in references.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.860
Sometimes they put in very
complex, deep knowledge.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:31.940
Is that good or bad?

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:33.830
I'll talk about
that in a moment.

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.800
And there's also other important
aspects to this process.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:39.440
Maybe you want to collect a
ton of data or very little data

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:40.410
that's high quality.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:41.990
So you've got this trade off.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:44.130
You also have to think
a lot about safety.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:46.260
The data that we
collected just now,

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:47.910
that's just capability's data.

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:51.210
It just makes models answer
thing like, what is CS336?

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:55.210
It does not help
us make our models

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:57.418
refuse malicious instructions
and things like that.

00:15:57.418 --> 00:15:59.960
So we have to think a little
bit about what that kind of data

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:02.900
looks like, too.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:06.800
I think length has always
been a big kind of gorilla

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:11.690
in the room issue for
all of these data sets.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.630
Back in 2023, when it
was very, very popular

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:17.630
to generate these kinds of
instruction tuning data sets,

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:20.720
there's a survey like Yizhong
Wang and others at U-Dub

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:22.310
came up with this
very nice survey,

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:24.530
looking over the many
different kinds of data sets

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:27.260
that were created
early in that year.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:30.720
And you see if you look at the
length of both the prompts,

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:33.620
so that's the inputs and the
responses, the completions,

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:38.450
you see really different lengths
of both inputs and outputs.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:40.910
And inputs are probably
a measure of complexity

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:43.110
of the task, in many ways.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.200
And then the outputs
are in some sense

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:48.300
a measure of how much
you push the annotators,

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:52.160
or if you used AI
generated responses.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:55.520
And one of the things that
you should be all aware of,

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:58.620
let's say you get put in charge
of making a new language model,

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:00.120
you're in charge
of post-training.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:02.870
Well, if you're
using human evals,

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:04.817
people have a strong
preference for lists.

00:17:04.817 --> 00:17:07.109
I mean, so do you-- actually,
if you use AI as a judge,

00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:09.260
they also have a strong
preference for lists.

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:11.820
And people have a very long
preference for outputs like 60%,

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:17.670
70%-ish preference for longer
outputs and so do AI judges.

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:20.359
And so this is a
little concerning

00:17:20.359 --> 00:17:22.490
because you want
to be optimizing

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:26.550
for not just the stylistic
content of your responses,

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.490
you ideally want to
be using post-training

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:30.920
to do things reduce
hallucinations

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:34.340
and actually make the model
hopefully more capable.

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:38.450
One thing that we do see is
that these factors are not

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:40.740
super relevant for
benchmark performance.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.700
So if you look at, for
example, MMLU performance,

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:47.720
despite the really big
variation in length

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:51.530
for a lot of these models, most
of the instruction tuning data

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:53.420
sets, like the
simple ones give you

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:58.075
boosts over the base model,
which is the very top row above.

00:17:58.075 --> 00:18:00.200
And so I think one of the
things that I'll say here

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:03.420
is chat style evaluations
have their place.

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:07.490
Chatbot Arena, AlpacaEval,
these kinds of automated evals

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:10.255
have their place in helping
understand user engagement

00:18:10.255 --> 00:18:11.130
and things like this.

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:13.700
But benchmarks also have
a very important place

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:15.800
because when you post-train,
you don't necessarily

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:18.260
want to be too affected by,
for example, length biases

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:20.510
and open ended
domains and so on.

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:22.887
And so you want to be really
careful of these effects.

00:18:22.887 --> 00:18:25.220
You want to have a different
diverse array of evaluation

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:28.800
strategies to try to
avoid those pitfalls.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:31.890
One other thing that I
think really trips people up

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:35.100
when they initially start
thinking about these things is

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.370
to say, oh, what I'm doing is
I want to collect high quality

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:41.760
data, and high quality data
has lots of deep knowledge

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.390
and has lots of citations.

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:45.630
That's a reasonable
thing to say.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:47.310
And I think
OpenAssistant I think

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:49.770
had a great example of this.

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:50.920
And so here's an example.

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:52.320
Input/output pair.

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:56.730
You've got a right introduction
about monopsony in economics.

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:58.590
And then there's
these references

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:00.420
on the response on the right.

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:02.890
So now, let's say
we have a model

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:06.000
and we fine-tune the model to
take the left side as input

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:09.040
and reproduce the
right side as output.

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:12.660
So you can think about
two different things

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:15.675
that this process is going
to do at the same time.

00:19:15.675 --> 00:19:17.550
So one of the things
that this is going to do

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:21.880
is it's going to associate
monopsony with that citation.

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:23.380
So it's learning new knowledge.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:24.400
So that is good.

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.520
So that is a
positive thing to do.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:29.920
But it's also going to do a
second thing at the same time,

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:32.350
which is this kind of
generalized thing of saying,

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:34.690
if you ask me a
complicated concept,

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:38.280
I had better finish the
output with a reference.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:40.752
And so it's basically
the first thing

00:19:40.752 --> 00:19:42.460
is teaching new
knowledge, which is good.

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.090
But the second thing here is
kind of teaching the model

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:46.260
to hallucinate.

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.270
If the model doesn't
already have somewhere

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:50.250
within its parameters,
an association

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:53.500
between monopsony
and this citation,

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:57.450
this Bivens and Mishel book,
what might happen instead

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:59.460
is it just learns
that, oh, what I should

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:01.390
do is whenever I have
a complicated input,

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:03.098
I should give a response
and then make up

00:20:03.098 --> 00:20:04.360
a reference at the very end.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.480
Those are two
competing explanations

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:09.570
for what is happening here.

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:13.080
And this is going to motivate,
in some ways, the second part

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:14.140
of this lecture.

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:17.140
John Schulman has this
kind of great talk,

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:22.200
I think he gave it at Berkeley,
where his argument is basically,

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:23.700
if you do this kind
of thing, you're

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:25.770
going to encourage the
model to hallucinate.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:27.570
The model doesn't
have the knowledge

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:29.520
of answering a
question, you force

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:30.965
it to answer that question.

00:20:30.965 --> 00:20:32.590
What it's going to
learn is, of course,

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:34.715
it will learn the knowledge
in some abstract sense,

00:20:34.715 --> 00:20:37.560
but it will also learn
the other aspect of I

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:40.220
just need to make something
up in order to type check what

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:41.470
the response should look like.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:42.470
Yes, there's a question.

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:43.450
I have a question.

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:46.680
So our human rights
designer paper, one writing

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:49.330
has a sense of, OK, I think I
need to add a citation here.

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:52.200
Let me search for relevant
citation either from memory

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.840
or let me actually
use a database.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:57.900
The fact that the LM is
learning that, OK, here I

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:00.540
should insert a
citation it's actually

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:02.440
a correct and desirable thing.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:05.490
And the fact that it's
a made up citation

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:08.730
is either a memory issue or
be something you can maybe

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:11.610
augment a tool usage in the--

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:14.970
I don't see why the behavior of
needing to add a citation itself

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:19.260
is problematic if you can either
fix the memory issue or the tool

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:20.200
usage issue.

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:20.700
Sure.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:21.575
I mean, I think the--

00:21:21.575 --> 00:21:23.910
OK, so to repeat the question,
the question was like--

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:26.000
I guess that was a more
comment than a question.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:30.255
Was that the learning to put in
a citation isn't a bad thing?

00:21:30.255 --> 00:21:31.880
I mean, maybe you
augment it with tools

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:33.300
and it'll actually give
the right citation.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:34.467
I mean, that's a fair point.

00:21:34.467 --> 00:21:36.620
But I think the thing
to maybe point out here

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:39.720
is the deeper conceptual--
or not conceptual,

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:42.290
the deeper issue
with token prediction

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.110
is that you're
teaching the model

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:47.460
to predict the right
kinds of tokens.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:50.960
And here, essentially, the
lesser of the two errors

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:54.320
is to say hallucinating
is less bad for my loss

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:56.630
than not making up
the reference at all.

00:21:56.630 --> 00:22:00.240
The structure of the response
always has to be fulfilled,

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:03.070
because you have to fill the
tokens in at the right places.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:05.280
Of course, at scale,
if you know the facts,

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:07.500
if you have the right
tools, those are good.

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:10.310
You want to make the
predictions on the right spaces.

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:16.130
But I do think this is very
indicative of this failure mode

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:18.980
that models can get into where
you're trying to get it to do

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:20.100
things that it can't.

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:22.690
If your SFT data is
just much more advanced

00:22:22.690 --> 00:22:26.530
than what your pre-trained
model naturally can do,

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:28.870
you run this risk of
teaching the model

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:30.880
this alternative
shortcut behavior

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:34.330
instead of teaching
models the right behavior.

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:34.840
Yeah.

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.350
So that's John Schulman.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:38.680
I think he makes a
fairly reasonable case

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:41.140
that this is one
of the reasons why

00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:43.683
on policy, RL, like
Reinforcement Learning

00:22:43.683 --> 00:22:45.100
style things is
an important thing

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:47.950
to do because you want to know
what the model already knows

00:22:47.950 --> 00:22:51.320
and only teach it those things
to avoid it hallucinating.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:54.100
And whenever it's encountering
some fact that it doesn't know,

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:56.870
then maybe you should change
your fine tuning data to say,

00:22:56.870 --> 00:22:59.350
oh, I don't know that fact,
instead of forcing the model

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:01.600
to try to answer.

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:07.840
And we see this on other
knowledge storage studies

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:10.450
as well, where
people have talked

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:13.480
about it's much
easier for models

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:19.330
to reproduce known facts
than to learn unknown facts

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:23.830
where it just takes a lot longer
for models to learn facts that

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:25.310
aren't shown in pre-training.

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:27.970
And this is matching
what you might

00:23:27.970 --> 00:23:30.050
expect from these phenomenon.

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:35.784
I think one of the
things that I'll

00:23:35.784 --> 00:23:38.200
summarize that with
is that there's

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:40.630
a very counterintuitive
phenomenon for instruction

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:44.380
tuning, which is that you can
have an instruction tuning data

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:47.750
set that is fully correct
and actually very rich,

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:49.760
but actually that might
not be good for your LM,

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:54.040
because it's going to teach your
language model to try to make up

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:55.898
facts to match that
depth of knowledge.

00:23:55.898 --> 00:23:57.940
That's always been, I
think, one of the arguments

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:01.360
for why you want to be really
careful with both distillation

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.600
data, where the teacher model
is stronger than your student

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:05.120
model.

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:08.060
And also, really
human annotation,

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:10.300
where the human might be
much more knowledgeable

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:11.210
than the model.

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:14.200
You want to be really careful
to make the model abstain nicely

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:16.480
when it doesn't know things.

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:19.400
And in principle, reinforcement
learning style correctness

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:19.950
could help.

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:21.710
And we'll talk about
that in a moment.

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:25.633
And optimizing this at the
instruction tuning level

00:24:25.633 --> 00:24:27.300
is just really messy
and very difficult.

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.450
I don't think people have
really nailed it down,

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:33.500
at least in the open
research literature.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:36.120
The other thing I want
to talk about briefly,

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:39.200
because I think this isn't
necessarily something that

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:42.560
can be solved with
instruction tuning alone,

00:24:42.560 --> 00:24:46.160
is to touch on safety and to
think a little bit about what

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.230
the trade-offs are here.

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:51.925
So we know language models
need some guardrails.

00:24:51.925 --> 00:24:53.550
They're deployed
straight to end users.

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:54.480
They're very capable.

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:56.900
So they might be used for
misinformation or for generating

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:59.000
things like scams or spam.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:03.600
And so there's a need for
safety tuning on these models.

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:05.120
And I think in
parallel with a lot

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:06.870
of the research on
instruction tuning,

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.800
there's been actually
quite a bit of work

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:12.140
studying safety tuning as well.

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:16.520
And I think some of the
early work in this area

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:20.030
has shown that even a small
amount of safety tuning

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:23.510
data that's mixed in to
instruction tuning process

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:26.660
can make models much
safer, paralleling

00:25:26.660 --> 00:25:29.150
a lot of the findings that
people had that actually,

00:25:29.150 --> 00:25:30.803
for instruction
tuning as well, if you

00:25:30.803 --> 00:25:32.220
a strong enough
pre-trained model,

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:34.460
even a small amount of
instruction tuning data

00:25:34.460 --> 00:25:36.110
can get you a lot of the way.

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:39.980
Not to say that's sufficient,
but actually that gets you

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.230
to a reasonable point.

00:25:42.230 --> 00:25:45.770
And I think the core
trade-off with safety tuning

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:48.810
that I'll touch on
in this brief section

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:52.070
is this trade-off between
refusing things and not

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:54.852
refusing too much.

00:25:54.852 --> 00:25:59.010
So there's always this thing of
if you have unsafe responses,

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.640
you want your safety tuned
model to just refuse to answer.

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:07.860
And then maybe you have these
other actually safe responses,

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:09.800
but things that look
like unsafe responses.

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:12.290
Like how can I kill
a Python process?

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:15.430
We all know that it's a
reasonable question to ask.

00:26:15.430 --> 00:26:18.977
But I guess if you don't
understand English very deeply,

00:26:18.977 --> 00:26:21.310
you're like, oh, killing,
killing sounds very dangerous.

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:24.510
So maybe I should refuse
to answer that question.

00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:28.030
So how can you make models
understand this nuance?

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:31.860
It's a very tricky thing to do,
purely in the instruction tuning

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:32.658
setting.

00:26:32.658 --> 00:26:34.200
And so a lot of what
people have done

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:37.410
is come up with carefully
curated small instruction tuning

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:41.100
data sets to try to
balance this trade off.

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:44.580
So even some research has shown
that even like 500 examples

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:47.530
can make models follow some
of the safety guidelines.

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:54.210
To put this together,
instruction tuning

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:56.410
is surprisingly powerful.

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:59.130
I think you would think that
given how powerful things

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:03.150
like ChatGPT are, that there's
actually a ton of complexity

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:05.000
into getting
anything that works.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:06.900
I think you'll find
that even if you

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:09.150
take a fairly standard
instruction tuning

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:12.210
data set, like OpenHermes
or OpenAssistant,

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:14.850
or any of these data sets,
and you take a base model

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:17.530
and you fine tune on it with
reasonable hyperparameters,

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:20.917
you'll get a model that behaves
a lot like Llama or ChatGPT.

00:27:20.917 --> 00:27:22.000
It won't be quite as good.

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:25.420
There's a lot of extra work
you need to do to optimize it.

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:27.900
But you can get pretty far.

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:30.570
The second thing
that's good to remember

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:33.510
is basically the notion
of high quality data

00:27:33.510 --> 00:27:35.040
is just very
complex, and you have

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:36.760
to reason about it
really carefully.

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:38.200
It's not obvious how to do.

00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:40.800
And then the last
thing is, actually

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:42.990
even a small amount of data
can have great leverage

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:47.100
at this stage in changing
how models behave.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:49.350
The last thing I want
to end this section on

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:53.370
is how to do this
instruction tuning.

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:56.970
There's a flippant answer to
this, which is, well, you've

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:58.710
got demonstrations.

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:01.293
Just put in the instruction
and the response

00:28:01.293 --> 00:28:02.710
and just do some
gradient descent.

00:28:02.710 --> 00:28:05.790
We all know how to do gradient
descent at this point.

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:09.070
And I think in most academic
settings, that's basically it.

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:09.780
You're done.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:14.340
You do your small scale gradient
descent, and you're done.

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:17.250
But I think if you're
at a frontier lab

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:19.350
and you've got more
compute and you've

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:22.620
got more money than
you what to do with,

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:25.560
then you've got a lot of compute
and you've got a lot of data.

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:28.320
And so you can scale this
whole process up quite a bit.

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:30.040
You can scale it up a lot.

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:33.240
And modern instruction
tuning pipelines

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:37.230
are starting to look a lot
like pre-training pipelines.

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:40.320
And so increasingly,
the boundaries

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:43.380
between pre-training
and instruction tuning

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:46.810
are just getting blurred
because if you think about it,

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:48.960
instruction tuning data
is still a sequence.

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:51.040
It's just a sequence of tokens.

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:54.100
And so I can throw that in
into my pre-training process.

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:56.400
And that's a totally
valid thing to do.

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:59.260
And so this is an
increasingly popular idea.

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:03.250
I think the close labs
don't tell us anything.

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:05.430
But I think the
things that people

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:08.430
tell me in bits and
pieces suggest that this

00:29:08.430 --> 00:29:09.960
is what they're doing.

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:13.400
A lot of the open groups from
China do basically this now.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:16.940
And so what you do is you have
your usual pre-training setting.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:18.680
You do pure pre-training.

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:20.750
And then what you're
going to do is

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:24.110
you're going to start mixing
in instruction tuning data

00:29:24.110 --> 00:29:25.020
into pre-training.

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:26.790
So the tail end of
your pre-training,

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:29.222
especially as you're
annealing the learning rate,

00:29:29.222 --> 00:29:31.430
you're going to start putting
in a lot of this higher

00:29:31.430 --> 00:29:33.945
quality data or
instruction tuning data.

00:29:33.945 --> 00:29:35.570
And then in the end,
you might actually

00:29:35.570 --> 00:29:38.070
do a second short
instruction tuning round.

00:29:38.070 --> 00:29:40.310
But maybe, this is smaller
because most of your data

00:29:40.310 --> 00:29:43.460
has already gone into the
second stage, what people call

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:44.690
mid-training.

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:46.940
And this is cool because
it lets you scale up

00:29:46.940 --> 00:29:49.307
without catastrophic
forgetting issues.

00:29:49.307 --> 00:29:51.140
You might get more
leverage out of your data

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:54.410
because it's integrated more
deeply into pre-training.

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:59.570
And to give you an example or a
sense of what this looks like--

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:01.640
and it's a bit of
a shame that data

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:05.600
mixes are often pretty
closely guarded secrets

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:07.130
by a lot of the groups.

00:30:07.130 --> 00:30:10.750
So I've taken this figure
from miniCPM, which

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:12.550
we've talked about before.

00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:15.910
Great paper from one of the
Chinese groups where basically,

00:30:15.910 --> 00:30:18.940
they have a two stage
training pipeline where

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:21.290
they have a first stage where
they do pure pre-training.

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:22.760
And if you look
at this pie chart,

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:24.710
this is all
pre-training data sets.

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:28.520
It's Common Crawl code,
pre-training, pile, dolma.

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:33.130
They've thrown it all
in into one big pie.

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:35.440
And then, they have
a second stage, which

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:36.800
they call the decay stage.

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:40.000
And so if you remember my
lecture on scaling laws,

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:42.860
I talked about WSD,
Warm up Stable Decay.

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:44.420
So that's the stable stage.

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:45.760
That's the decay stage.

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:47.600
And the decay stage,
what have we got?

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:52.100
We've got Wikipedia, what people
might call high quality data.

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:54.350
We've got still the pre-training
stuff mixed in there.

00:30:54.350 --> 00:30:56.630
So it's not pure
post-training data.

00:30:56.630 --> 00:30:59.420
But then, if you look at the
right, we've got Code SFT,

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:02.870
we've got Chinese Books,
we've got UltraChat,

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:05.170
we've got Stack Exchange
Question &amp; Answering,

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:08.950
and Evol-Instruct
and OSS-Instruct,

00:31:08.950 --> 00:31:11.170
and all sorts of other things.

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:14.110
So those are all kind
of instruction tuning

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:16.270
or instruction tuning
adjacent data sets

00:31:16.270 --> 00:31:20.770
that you've thrown in onto the
second half of pre-training.

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:24.790
And I think used by most
models today and many CPM

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:26.590
and other derived--

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:29.440
LMs that are derived from
that lineage of models

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:32.170
have definitely publicized this.

00:31:32.170 --> 00:31:35.930
I think it's extremely
effective to do this.

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:39.010
And so I think everyone
has been following this.

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:43.420
One last commentary I'll make
before we move on to RLHF here,

00:31:43.420 --> 00:31:48.500
is that this whole
process makes it very,

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:51.880
very difficult to reason about
pre-trained models versus post

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:52.870
trained models.

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:56.657
If you look at recent
releases from Qwen or whatever

00:31:56.657 --> 00:31:58.240
other companies that
you're looking at

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:01.450
and they say base
model, that base model

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:04.113
is probably at the
end of this process.

00:32:04.113 --> 00:32:06.530
And so it has basically gone
through an instruction tuning

00:32:06.530 --> 00:32:10.280
phase, implicitly through
its training process.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:12.920
We don't exactly know
what the mixes are

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:14.760
for a lot of these
closed models,

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:17.690
but it does actually mean
that I think the term base

00:32:17.690 --> 00:32:21.680
models is increasingly
questionable what that really

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:22.670
means.

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:25.865
So that's my side comment
that is useful for if you're

00:32:25.865 --> 00:32:26.990
thinking about base models.

00:32:26.990 --> 00:32:27.875
Yes?

00:32:27.875 --> 00:32:30.145
[INAUDIBLE] is the
data mixture you

00:32:30.145 --> 00:32:31.520
changed during
[INAUDIBLE] stage,

00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:34.790
like, when do you have
[INAUDIBLE], that's when you get

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:36.360
these big decreases in loss.

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:40.790
Is that directly [INAUDIBLE]
in that last stage?

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:42.500
Yeah, so that's right.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:45.410
That was the motivation
for a lot of the two phase

00:32:45.410 --> 00:32:47.610
training for these groups.

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:51.260
They basically use essentially
the large drop in loss

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:55.700
as a way to try to anneal the
model into the right mode.

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:59.240
I think there's
increasing studies

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:02.162
into what's the optimal
point at which to switch?

00:33:02.162 --> 00:33:04.370
And I think it's a little
bit more nuanced than that.

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:05.580
But I think it's a first order.

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:07.163
This has been a very
effective recipe.

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:13.670
Between blending [INAUDIBLE]
catastrophic forgetting,

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:16.490
or I'm trying to think about
the incentivized citation thing

00:33:16.490 --> 00:33:17.750
from earlier.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:19.310
Does this also have that?

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:19.850
Yeah.

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:22.380
So I guess there were two
questions packed in one.

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:25.310
But the question was,
is this primarily

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:27.180
for catastrophic forgetting?

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:30.890
And also does this help
with the citations issue?

00:33:30.890 --> 00:33:33.810
So to answer the
second part first,

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:35.840
I think it doesn't help
with the citation issue

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:39.960
because just as a
type signature thing,

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:42.680
the only things that can
help with the citation issue

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:45.650
is if you know what
facts the model knows.

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:48.110
So you have to either ensure
that the model, like always,

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:51.570
knows the citation facts before
you show it the SFT data.

00:33:51.570 --> 00:33:55.670
Or you have to check to see if
the model knows it and then show

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:59.130
it that data if it does know
it, which this doesn't do.

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:01.680
This will always unconditionally
put in those data points,

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:03.430
whether or not that
citation is learned.

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:06.288
So it no way of fixing
that adaptively.

00:34:06.288 --> 00:34:07.830
Catastrophic forgetting
wise, I think

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:12.270
that is one of the motivations
that if you have so much SFT

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:15.485
data, your trade offs
are pretty tricky.

00:34:15.485 --> 00:34:16.860
Because unless
you're going to do

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:19.985
this kind of almost pre-training
mixed in with post-training,

00:34:19.985 --> 00:34:21.610
you have to think
about regularization.

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:23.580
You have to think
about tiny step sizes

00:34:23.580 --> 00:34:25.900
to avoid messing up
your pre-training.

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:29.190
And so I think this
is partially motivated

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:33.130
by the catastrophic
forgetting adjacent issues.

00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:36.810
It keeps the model more general.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:39.659
Yes.

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:44.190
With the John Schulman
example for hallucination,

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:46.949
is that if the model
doesn't no longer

00:34:46.949 --> 00:34:51.070
reference that's included in the
training data, that analysis,

00:34:51.070 --> 00:34:53.909
or if it does that
fast, then it would not

00:34:53.909 --> 00:34:55.080
have the same behavior?

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:55.659
That's right.

00:34:55.659 --> 00:34:59.520
That's the claim,
that if the model did

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:04.530
know this citation
that's right here, then

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:07.560
the model wouldn't necessarily
out of these two competing

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:08.800
mechanisms.

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:12.040
What it would learn is, oh,
whenever I see this example,

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:16.140
I should retrieve my knowledge
about Bivens and Mishel, L

00:35:16.140 --> 00:35:18.600
and then use that as a citation.

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:22.080
I think the reality of
this is that it's always

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:23.190
very complicated.

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:25.180
What does it mean for
a model to something?

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:27.100
How reliably does
it know something?

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:29.130
And so these two
kind of mechanisms

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.812
are always maybe in
superposition for a model.

00:35:31.812 --> 00:35:34.270
It's really just a question
of, which one is more dominant?

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:36.160
If a model just has
no idea about this,

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:39.010
it's probably two
that is more dominant.

00:35:39.010 --> 00:35:41.320
Whereas I think if the
model knows that reliably,

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:42.930
it's more likely
that it's just going

00:35:42.930 --> 00:35:44.430
to learn the correct
citation rather

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:47.610
than encouraging broad
general hallucinations.

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:48.840
Yes.

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:51.270
Have people tried
putting into all

00:35:51.270 --> 00:35:54.160
of the pre-data,
some kind of thoughts

00:35:54.160 --> 00:35:57.730
tokens that tell the model,
oh, this looks like a fact.

00:35:57.730 --> 00:35:59.190
Let me check if I
actually know it.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:02.850
And so I'm going to query
myself and check if I'm

00:36:02.850 --> 00:36:04.030
getting consistent answers.

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:06.270
And if so, I'll print out.

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:08.400
So that in the entire
training process

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:12.696
it has a thought process
of I need to check myself.

00:36:12.696 --> 00:36:14.350
That is a very interesting idea.

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:17.670
So just to repeat
it, has anyone done

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:19.793
something where you
put in thought tokens,

00:36:19.793 --> 00:36:21.210
where the model
is checking itself

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:24.300
for its knowledge of facts as it
trains or something like that?

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:25.360
Is that roughly right?

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:25.860
Yeah.

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:30.240
Yeah, so depending on how
you interpret or implement

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:32.130
that exact idea, it
starts to look a lot

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:34.020
like reinforcement learning.

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:39.330
Because, for example, there's
a method called Quiet-STaR

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:41.730
from some folks here, Noah
Goodman and Eric Zelikman

00:36:41.730 --> 00:36:45.700
and others have done this,
where they do essentially,

00:36:45.700 --> 00:36:48.720
they try to learn the
thinking process of a model

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:52.993
by predicting what happens
on the answer token.

00:36:52.993 --> 00:36:54.910
And then based on whether
or not it's correct,

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:58.860
it tries to reinforce the model
to have good thought process.

00:36:58.860 --> 00:37:01.710
Actually, even closer analogy
to this is [? Star, ?] which

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:02.860
is the original paper.

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:05.263
Which is if the model
gets something correct,

00:37:05.263 --> 00:37:06.930
then that thinking
process gets fed back

00:37:06.930 --> 00:37:08.200
into the model training.

00:37:08.200 --> 00:37:09.810
And if it's wrong,
then it doesn't.

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:11.970
And it's very similar
to what you're

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:14.130
proposing, which is
to adaptively train

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:16.620
the model based on
correctness of its knowledge

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:18.030
or whatever else.

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:20.850
I'm proposing just to give
it thought processes that

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:23.790
say, let me make use of
this tool that tracks myself

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:27.193
and that can later, at some
point, change what that tool is.

00:37:27.193 --> 00:37:29.860
But the point is, that tool will
come with a response that says,

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:31.690
yes, I do have the
knowledge, no, I don't.

00:37:31.690 --> 00:37:34.380
And then you would actually see
that knowledge gets printed out

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:36.150
or doesn't depending on, I see.

00:37:36.150 --> 00:37:38.730
So, in this tool
use example, are

00:37:38.730 --> 00:37:41.940
you imagining that
the fact would

00:37:41.940 --> 00:37:44.650
get replaced by a tool call or
will the fact still be there?

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:47.160
I think the key question
is, do you force the model

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:48.880
to predict the fact tokens?

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:50.850
Or do you just
force it to predict,

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:54.380
use a tool to look it
up on Google token?

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:54.880
No.

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:57.297
No, no, the tool just checks
if the model knows it.

00:37:57.297 --> 00:37:59.630
But you don't know what the
tool-- how you implement it.

00:37:59.630 --> 00:38:02.410
But you see, if the process
is, yes, I do know it,

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:07.060
then there will actually be
some response printed out

00:38:07.060 --> 00:38:08.180
in the free training data.

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:10.410
And if the tool says
no, I don't know it,

00:38:10.410 --> 00:38:13.120
then the free training
data will just say,

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:16.580
actually, I don't know
this, I'm going to skip it.

00:38:16.580 --> 00:38:20.030
So I think that's hard because
when you do pre-training,

00:38:20.030 --> 00:38:23.410
you have to know whether the
fact to know whether to take

00:38:23.410 --> 00:38:25.250
losses on that knowledge token.

00:38:25.250 --> 00:38:27.380
You can't defer it
to inference time,

00:38:27.380 --> 00:38:29.380
because you have to decide
whether or not you're

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:30.710
going to take gradient steps.

00:38:30.710 --> 00:38:33.520
And the other logistical
difficulty here

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:36.700
is during pre-training time,
you have a static data set

00:38:36.700 --> 00:38:38.610
or for computational
reasons, you

00:38:38.610 --> 00:38:39.890
would want a static data set.

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:41.307
And if you have a
static data set,

00:38:41.307 --> 00:38:43.220
you can't adaptively do updates.

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:45.370
Anything that solves
the hallucination

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:47.840
problem has to be
reactive of the form,

00:38:47.840 --> 00:38:49.060
what does the model know?

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:51.140
And then do I take
updates on this or not?

00:38:51.140 --> 00:38:53.380
At the pre-training stage,
that's very difficult

00:38:53.380 --> 00:38:55.940
unless you're doing RL style
stuff at pre-training scale,

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:57.920
which would get you
very close to that,

00:38:57.920 --> 00:39:01.190
but still very difficult.
I'm happy to follow up,

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:05.080
but I think hopefully
that answers the question.

00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:06.190
Oh, there's more, yes.

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:15.760
In case of FLAN,
in [INAUDIBLE], we

00:39:15.760 --> 00:39:17.290
haven't [INAUDIBLE] the emoji.

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:20.710
But if [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:20.710 --> 00:39:22.660
emojis [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:24.190
Where it comes to
[INAUDIBLE] that

00:39:24.190 --> 00:39:28.245
when you start to control the
model and the result model,

00:39:28.245 --> 00:39:29.620
you will have a
lot of the gaming

00:39:29.620 --> 00:39:34.190
systems, similar technology
partners at the [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:34.690
Yeah.

00:39:34.690 --> 00:39:36.640
OK, so the question was, if
a pre-training we don't see

00:39:36.640 --> 00:39:38.890
emojis but a post-training
we put in a bunch of emojis

00:39:38.890 --> 00:39:40.480
at the end, what will happen?

00:39:40.480 --> 00:39:43.660
It depends on the structure
of the emojis, I guess.

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:46.900
If the emojis are dependent on
the inputs in a very complex way

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:48.410
and that's very
difficult to learn,

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:50.750
maybe what the model
will learn is, well,

00:39:50.750 --> 00:39:53.030
in post-training, what I
saw was a bunch of emojis.

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:54.700
I don't have enough
data or training

00:39:54.700 --> 00:39:56.250
to know what the
complex pattern is,

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:58.750
so the model will just learn
to put a bunch of random emojis

00:39:58.750 --> 00:40:00.130
at the end.

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:03.750
If there's no pattern, if
there's no complex dependence,

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:06.250
then maybe the model will learn
to do the right thing, which

00:40:06.250 --> 00:40:09.280
is just to put a bunch of
random emojis at the end.

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:13.390
Really, the key way to
think about the SFT issues

00:40:13.390 --> 00:40:16.810
is instruction
tuning will reliably

00:40:16.810 --> 00:40:19.420
teach the style of the
output, like the type

00:40:19.420 --> 00:40:20.630
signature of the output.

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:23.500
And the model will most likely
follow that type signature

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:24.475
at the very least.

00:40:24.475 --> 00:40:27.100
And the real question is, do you
have enough instruction tuning

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:28.880
data that you could do
something more than that?

00:40:28.880 --> 00:40:30.800
And that's the more
complex open question.

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:32.630
So in your emoji case,
at the very least,

00:40:32.630 --> 00:40:34.900
you'll get a bunch of
emojis, whether those emojis

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:36.872
are the right emojis,
open question.

00:40:36.872 --> 00:40:38.830
It depends on how much
instruction tuning data,

00:40:38.830 --> 00:40:42.550
depends on pre-training,
and so on and so forth.

00:40:42.550 --> 00:40:43.960
Yes.

00:40:43.960 --> 00:40:45.880
I was wondering
earlier in the lecture

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:48.910
I think it was said that the
post-training part doesn't

00:40:48.910 --> 00:40:50.900
really teach a lot
of new knowledge.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:52.260
It's mostly about styles.

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:55.430
And then the line
gets more blurry.

00:40:55.430 --> 00:40:57.710
This is me training stage.

00:40:57.710 --> 00:41:00.750
So I don't think you should
like the miniCPM paper.

00:41:00.750 --> 00:41:04.490
So in this view like
scenario, like the me

00:41:04.490 --> 00:41:08.600
training part could also
use some new world knowledge

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.800
within the model.

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:10.620
Yeah, that's right.

00:41:10.620 --> 00:41:14.750
So I guess the question
was like, if I rephrase it,

00:41:14.750 --> 00:41:16.497
can instruction tuning
essentially teach

00:41:16.497 --> 00:41:17.330
new world knowledge?

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:18.788
Because mid training
blurs the line

00:41:18.788 --> 00:41:20.737
between pre-training
and instruction tuning.

00:41:20.737 --> 00:41:22.320
And we pre-training
teaches knowledge.

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:23.490
So why not instruction tuning?

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:24.532
And I think that's right.

00:41:24.532 --> 00:41:27.920
That in some ways instruction
tuning if it's scaled up enough

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:30.270
and it's diverse enough
will teach knowledge.

00:41:30.270 --> 00:41:32.360
But I think instruction
tuning and it's

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:35.700
smaller like non-mid
training form,

00:41:35.700 --> 00:41:38.060
it is very difficult to
have the scale and diversity

00:41:38.060 --> 00:41:42.170
of data needed to reliably
teach various facts.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:44.360
I think modern
mid-training is starting

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:47.730
to become a different game, but
it's still an emerging object,

00:41:47.730 --> 00:41:49.250
I think.

00:41:49.250 --> 00:41:49.820
Cool.

00:41:49.820 --> 00:41:53.850
OK, so now, we get
to the part two.

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:56.330
So part one this
is the quick intro

00:41:56.330 --> 00:41:58.820
to instruction tuning in SFT.

00:41:58.820 --> 00:42:02.580
Now, we get to reinforcement
learning from human feedback,

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:05.660
the RL part of this lecture.

00:42:05.660 --> 00:42:09.110
And conceptually, and I'm
going to take it slow here,

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:12.480
because I think this is an
important conceptual transition.

00:42:12.480 --> 00:42:14.810
We're going to move from
the world of, I think,

00:42:14.810 --> 00:42:18.060
generative modeling
at the very top here,

00:42:18.060 --> 00:42:21.177
which is there's a very simple
goal in this world, which

00:42:21.177 --> 00:42:22.010
is there's a p-star.

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:25.490
P-star is a reference
distribution from which

00:42:25.490 --> 00:42:26.850
completions are drawn.

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:28.370
That reference
distribution probably

00:42:28.370 --> 00:42:32.450
looks some mixture
of internet data

00:42:32.450 --> 00:42:34.230
as well as annotator
written data.

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:35.952
But there exists
some abstract p-star

00:42:35.952 --> 00:42:37.160
that we're trying to imitate.

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:39.290
That's all there is to it.

00:42:39.290 --> 00:42:41.047
So this is a pure
generative modeling.

00:42:41.047 --> 00:42:43.130
Now, we're going to move
to the second perspective

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:45.150
now, which is RLHF.

00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:47.690
And in this world,
I no longer care

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:49.400
about matching any distribution.

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:52.820
So probabilistic perspectives
don't really entirely go out

00:42:52.820 --> 00:42:55.333
the window, but you want to be
careful about adopting those,

00:42:55.333 --> 00:42:56.750
because really,
what we're looking

00:42:56.750 --> 00:43:00.200
for is we're really just
looking for some policy, p of y

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:03.210
given x, such that we
maximize our rewards.

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:05.210
There's some reward
function, r of y

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:08.490
and x, that take in both
my completion and my prompt

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:09.780
and then it gives me a reward.

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:11.810
And all I'm looking
for is any policy that

00:43:11.810 --> 00:43:13.952
gives me good rewards.

00:43:13.952 --> 00:43:17.270
And so now, LMs
are not necessarily

00:43:17.270 --> 00:43:19.380
a model for some
underlying distribution.

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:22.610
They are policies that
give us good rewards.

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:26.300
And so why would
we go and do RLHF?

00:43:26.300 --> 00:43:28.890
There are two reasons
that we might do this.

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:31.980
One of them is on
the top one an SFT.

00:43:31.980 --> 00:43:34.490
In order to do this
process of imitation,

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:36.690
we have to get
samples from p-star.

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:39.050
And that can be quite expensive.

00:43:39.050 --> 00:43:41.150
And the second one,
all we need to do

00:43:41.150 --> 00:43:44.430
is get measurements
of rewards, R.

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:47.650
And SFT data can just be
really, really expensive.

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:51.630
This is like a caricature
of various costs

00:43:51.630 --> 00:43:53.310
that you might have
in different stages.

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:56.303
You have some compute costs
when you train your base model,

00:43:56.303 --> 00:43:57.720
and then you've
got-- you're doing

00:43:57.720 --> 00:43:59.260
supervised learning like SFT.

00:43:59.260 --> 00:44:00.718
And then you're
going to go collect

00:44:00.718 --> 00:44:03.790
a bunch of pairwise feedback
and do RL and do evaluation,

00:44:03.790 --> 00:44:05.100
and so on.

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:09.570
So when you do
this right, the SFT

00:44:09.570 --> 00:44:11.140
is just really,
really expensive.

00:44:11.140 --> 00:44:15.430
You're getting expert people to
write very long form responses.

00:44:15.430 --> 00:44:18.630
And you saw how annoying
and difficult that was.

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:20.910
And Frontier labs are going
to be spending millions

00:44:20.910 --> 00:44:22.210
on this post training data.

00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:25.290
Well, maybe there's a
nicer way of collecting

00:44:25.290 --> 00:44:26.800
data that makes models better.

00:44:26.800 --> 00:44:28.428
So that's one
argument for why we're

00:44:28.428 --> 00:44:29.970
going to do all the
things that we're

00:44:29.970 --> 00:44:32.580
going to do in this second
part of this lecture.

00:44:32.580 --> 00:44:36.300
There's also a second reason
that is equally or maybe even

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:42.240
more important,
that I think people

00:44:42.240 --> 00:44:44.520
do this kind of RL training.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:47.520
And one of the things
that's very interesting

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:51.510
is people don't always
agree with themselves

00:44:51.510 --> 00:44:52.660
about what is good.

00:44:52.660 --> 00:44:56.220
So if you ask somebody
to write a summary,

00:44:56.220 --> 00:44:57.390
they can write one summary.

00:44:57.390 --> 00:45:01.350
And then you ask them to
compare their own summaries

00:45:01.350 --> 00:45:04.770
to LM-written summaries,
there's a good amount

00:45:04.770 --> 00:45:08.860
of people that will actually
prefer LM-written summaries.

00:45:08.860 --> 00:45:10.800
And this was a really
surprising result

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:15.960
from one of my student's papers,
I guess two years back now,

00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:19.440
where we were benchmarking
summarization systems.

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:21.820
And there was one person,
who is, of course,

00:45:21.820 --> 00:45:26.280
anonymized by annotator 1 who
wrote a bunch of summaries

00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:29.760
and they actually preferred
the AI summaries actually

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.510
significantly more
than their own.

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:34.230
And they're like a freelance
expert writer or something.

00:45:34.230 --> 00:45:35.605
And we went and
interviewed them.

00:45:35.605 --> 00:45:38.063
And they were like, yeah, when
you asked me to write stuff,

00:45:38.063 --> 00:45:40.720
I just felt like I had to write
more with flowery language.

00:45:40.720 --> 00:45:44.100
But then I read the AI ones
and they just read better.

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:46.650
And I'm sure you've had
similar experiences, where

00:45:46.650 --> 00:45:49.900
you look at the output
and it is actually

00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:52.150
different from your own
assessment of how to generate.

00:45:52.150 --> 00:45:56.220
And so those are just cheaper
to verify than generate,

00:45:56.220 --> 00:46:00.210
but actually maybe
higher quality to verify

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:01.120
than to generate.

00:46:01.120 --> 00:46:03.720
And so there's this
generator validator gap.

00:46:03.720 --> 00:46:07.710
So we're going to cover
different aspects of this RLHF

00:46:07.710 --> 00:46:09.090
process.

00:46:09.090 --> 00:46:11.637
We're going to talk
about how we collect data

00:46:11.637 --> 00:46:14.220
and what are things you should
worry about if you're in charge

00:46:14.220 --> 00:46:16.470
of RLHF data collection.

00:46:16.470 --> 00:46:18.910
And we're going to talk
about how we do RLHF.

00:46:18.910 --> 00:46:21.630
I'm going to talk about two
representative algorithms, PPO

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:22.710
and DPO.

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:27.090
I'll defer some of the more
detailed explanations of PPO

00:46:27.090 --> 00:46:30.090
to the next lecture,
just for space reasons.

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:32.970
And then finally, we'll
end with some things

00:46:32.970 --> 00:46:36.060
to worry about, almost like
pitfalls of RLHF at the very end

00:46:36.060 --> 00:46:36.560
here.

00:46:39.146 --> 00:46:41.300
So how do we get
pairwise feedback?

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:44.110
So pairwise feedback is--

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:47.710
oh, sorry, I'll go back here and
just take it a little slower.

00:46:47.710 --> 00:46:51.890
So when we do this second part
of this InstructGPT process,

00:46:51.890 --> 00:46:52.970
how does this work?

00:46:52.970 --> 00:46:55.820
Well, we have the model
generate its own outputs.

00:46:55.820 --> 00:46:57.950
These are rollouts in RL terms.

00:46:57.950 --> 00:47:01.430
And then we're going to compare
these different outputs.

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:04.630
And although the four
outputs are shown here

00:47:04.630 --> 00:47:08.360
in standard settings, you often
just have a pair of outputs.

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:11.080
And let's say, we have A
and B. All I want to know,

00:47:11.080 --> 00:47:12.800
is A better than B or not?

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:16.420
And then given these
pairwise feedbacks,

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:19.390
I'm going to train a reward
model that can essentially

00:47:19.390 --> 00:47:23.680
internally give every single
output a scalar value and then

00:47:23.680 --> 00:47:25.970
just use that to do
reinforcement learning.

00:47:25.970 --> 00:47:28.010
This reward model
is now my rewards.

00:47:28.010 --> 00:47:31.360
And I want my model to maximize
those rewards, fairly hopefully

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:32.930
simple pipeline.

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:36.880
So how do we collect
pairwise feedback data?

00:47:36.880 --> 00:47:41.200
Well, the obvious
simple thing to do

00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:44.060
is just say, OK, I'm just
going to make some web app.

00:47:44.060 --> 00:47:46.320
I've got two different
AI responses,

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:48.070
and you're going to
have a little four way

00:47:48.070 --> 00:47:50.750
checkbox that checks
which response is better.

00:47:50.750 --> 00:47:53.860
I took this from one of
the studies that we did.

00:47:53.860 --> 00:47:58.040
A lot of pairwise feedback
responses look similar to this.

00:47:58.040 --> 00:48:00.220
But I thought, one
thing that would

00:48:00.220 --> 00:48:04.270
be helpful and useful into
actually getting a sense of what

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:06.370
does this look like for real?

00:48:06.370 --> 00:48:11.260
I have gone and dug up examples
of annotation guidelines

00:48:11.260 --> 00:48:15.430
from different papers
and different places,

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:17.000
talking about this process.

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:20.180
So if we look at the
InstructGPT guideline,

00:48:20.180 --> 00:48:24.685
this is one of the
very few, I would say,

00:48:24.685 --> 00:48:26.650
released materials from
one of these companies

00:48:26.650 --> 00:48:29.620
describing their
annotation guidelines.

00:48:29.620 --> 00:48:31.750
They say, OK, your
job is to evaluate

00:48:31.750 --> 00:48:34.340
these outputs to ensure that
they're helpful, truthful,

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:35.350
and harmless.

00:48:35.350 --> 00:48:37.430
So those are their
three pillars.

00:48:37.430 --> 00:48:41.820
You've got helpfulness, which
is writing in clear language.

00:48:41.820 --> 00:48:44.010
Answer the question
they mean to ask,

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:46.310
being sensitive to
internationality like

00:48:46.310 --> 00:48:48.530
if someone says
football, they shouldn't

00:48:48.530 --> 00:48:50.360
assume American football.

00:48:50.360 --> 00:48:53.750
If it's too confusing,
ask for clarifications.

00:48:53.750 --> 00:48:56.960
I want you to be truthful
and not hallucinate outputs.

00:48:56.960 --> 00:49:01.850
And by harmless, you should
be not toxic and be very nice

00:49:01.850 --> 00:49:04.130
and not say NSW things.

00:49:04.130 --> 00:49:06.710
All fairly reasonable
things, but you

00:49:06.710 --> 00:49:11.900
can see how there's the
interplay between things

00:49:11.900 --> 00:49:14.700
like the model spec which
OpenAI publishes publicly.

00:49:14.700 --> 00:49:17.660
Then there's a very detailed
annotation guideline, which

00:49:17.660 --> 00:49:19.110
this is not that detailed.

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:20.917
It's probably much
bigger in practice

00:49:20.917 --> 00:49:23.250
where you would write down
these kinds of bullet points.

00:49:23.250 --> 00:49:25.490
You would hand
this to annotators,

00:49:25.490 --> 00:49:28.560
and then they would go
and make annotations.

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:31.830
This was InstructGPT is not
even kind of production grade.

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:36.890
This is early days, but you see
how this process kind of works.

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:40.160
The other interesting
example, you

00:49:40.160 --> 00:49:42.973
can go look this one up
later if the actual text is

00:49:42.973 --> 00:49:45.390
too small because I'm not going
to read through all of it.

00:49:45.390 --> 00:49:47.810
I'll just touch on it a bit.

00:49:47.810 --> 00:49:49.910
There's actually
a leaked version

00:49:49.910 --> 00:49:53.210
of the actual true annotation
guideline, apparently,

00:49:53.210 --> 00:49:54.770
for Google Bard.

00:49:54.770 --> 00:49:58.130
That, I think, was part
of some news story.

00:49:58.130 --> 00:50:01.710
And you can see very, very
similar things happening here.

00:50:01.710 --> 00:50:04.410
We've got on the top left
box like helpfulness.

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:06.540
You should address the
intent of the user's prompt.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:08.310
You should adhere
to any requirements.

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:10.670
Don't have misleading
information-- very similar

00:50:10.670 --> 00:50:12.710
to the InstructGPT setup.

00:50:12.710 --> 00:50:17.480
We've got actually here a style
box, which is what kind of style

00:50:17.480 --> 00:50:19.220
are good or bad?

00:50:19.220 --> 00:50:22.220
And then we've got
different rating scales

00:50:22.220 --> 00:50:25.490
for the different responses.

00:50:25.490 --> 00:50:29.180
And if I remember right, I
think the Google Bard folks

00:50:29.180 --> 00:50:31.790
I think gets like a
minute per question

00:50:31.790 --> 00:50:36.755
to be doing this task,
which is quite difficult.

00:50:36.755 --> 00:50:41.930
And then for InstructGPT, they
go through Scale and Upwork

00:50:41.930 --> 00:50:44.870
and they collect about
data from about 40 people.

00:50:44.870 --> 00:50:48.840
This is really, really
tiny by today's standards.

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:52.250
But hopefully, you get a
sense what types of groups

00:50:52.250 --> 00:50:53.830
are being involved here.

00:50:53.830 --> 00:50:58.640
So this is the second part
of our interactive exercise,

00:50:58.640 --> 00:51:00.170
OK, cool.

00:51:00.170 --> 00:51:02.510
So that's five minutes.

00:51:02.510 --> 00:51:07.640
I guess to take a straw
poll, I think about 27

00:51:07.640 --> 00:51:10.110
of you managed to complete
this, which is great,

00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:12.830
thank you for your
participation.

00:51:12.830 --> 00:51:18.110
How many of you managed to
fact check all the facts?

00:51:18.110 --> 00:51:19.740
So for one of the questions.

00:51:19.740 --> 00:51:20.360
Yeah.

00:51:20.360 --> 00:51:22.260
The [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:22.260 --> 00:51:22.760
Yeah.

00:51:22.760 --> 00:51:25.980
The longer expression,
completely factually wrong.

00:51:25.980 --> 00:51:26.774
Right.

00:51:26.774 --> 00:51:28.098
Yes, yes.

00:51:28.098 --> 00:51:28.640
That's right.

00:51:28.640 --> 00:51:31.210
So how many of you
managed to fact check?

00:51:31.210 --> 00:51:34.057
Any or all of the facts?

00:51:34.057 --> 00:51:35.390
You manage to fact check things?

00:51:35.390 --> 00:51:35.890
Just one.

00:51:35.890 --> 00:51:37.122
Just one, OK.

00:51:37.122 --> 00:51:40.483
How many of you managed to check
the math in the five minutes?

00:51:40.483 --> 00:51:41.900
OK, so there are
a couple people--

00:51:41.900 --> 00:51:43.220
OK, good.

00:51:43.220 --> 00:51:45.640
That makes me happy.

00:51:45.640 --> 00:51:49.420
So the point of this
exercise, partially, I mean,

00:51:49.420 --> 00:51:50.920
I think maybe you
could have guessed

00:51:50.920 --> 00:51:52.720
what was going to happen here.

00:51:52.720 --> 00:51:55.612
The shorter ones, most
of them are essentially

00:51:55.612 --> 00:51:57.070
taking the longer
ones and actually

00:51:57.070 --> 00:52:00.860
just removing the hallucinations
to the best of my ability.

00:52:00.860 --> 00:52:03.455
And so for the most
part, basically

00:52:03.455 --> 00:52:05.080
for those of you that
picked the longer

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:08.797
one are picking the slightly
longer but hallucinated ones.

00:52:08.797 --> 00:52:10.880
I don't quite remember
which of the pair of games,

00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:14.290
ones that are hallucinated,
but many of them are.

00:52:14.290 --> 00:52:16.850
And so we see
strong disagreement.

00:52:16.850 --> 00:52:19.090
And actually the longer
one gets more votes

00:52:19.090 --> 00:52:20.690
despite having strong
hallucinations.

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:22.148
This one, I think,
both is correct.

00:52:22.148 --> 00:52:24.730
So B is probably
the better choice.

00:52:24.730 --> 00:52:27.280
The two math ones,
I think you can also

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:30.420
back out by the unnaturalness
of this construction.

00:52:30.420 --> 00:52:33.090
The one that's more
conclusive actually

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:35.560
is going to the
wrong conclusion.

00:52:35.560 --> 00:52:40.200
This is not mathematically
fully correct here.

00:52:40.200 --> 00:52:44.100
And so you probably
should find it

00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:47.880
difficult to try to do these
judgments this quickly.

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:50.670
There are very, very
strong challenges

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:54.030
in collecting this kind
of pairwise feedback

00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:55.630
at very, very large scales.

00:52:55.630 --> 00:52:57.330
Just because even
though you only

00:52:57.330 --> 00:52:59.940
have to verify if I'm
showing you a math problem

00:52:59.940 --> 00:53:04.080
or I'm showing you something
like a very strong, factually

00:53:04.080 --> 00:53:08.580
laden text, you're going to have
to basically break this down

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:12.600
into claims and check each
one to whether one is wrong

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:13.690
and one is right.

00:53:13.690 --> 00:53:15.210
So this is just
like a very labor

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:17.410
intensive and difficult task.

00:53:17.410 --> 00:53:20.990
And I gave you five minutes
because essentially, I

00:53:20.990 --> 00:53:23.790
think the news story that
the Google Bard article was

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:28.140
associated with was basically
that the annotators are given

00:53:28.140 --> 00:53:31.830
one minute per example, and
there was no big complaints

00:53:31.830 --> 00:53:33.990
that this is not enough
for us to judge the safety

00:53:33.990 --> 00:53:36.280
and factual accuracy
of the model,

00:53:36.280 --> 00:53:38.670
and you hopefully
somewhat agree that it

00:53:38.670 --> 00:53:42.000
is a very difficult task.

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:47.800
OK, the lessons here, going
back to the flow of the lecture,

00:53:47.800 --> 00:53:51.570
it's very hard to get high
quality, verifiable annotators.

00:53:51.570 --> 00:53:54.583
I think you are in many ways
pretty high on the bar of people

00:53:54.583 --> 00:53:57.000
that are actually motivated
to do this because you're just

00:53:57.000 --> 00:53:59.670
doing it because I asked you to
not because I'm paying you to

00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:01.680
or to get grades.

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:04.450
It's very difficult to get
people to check correctness,

00:54:04.450 --> 00:54:06.750
especially under
time constraints.

00:54:06.750 --> 00:54:09.798
And the last one, I don't
know if those of you

00:54:09.798 --> 00:54:10.840
who are doing this right.

00:54:10.840 --> 00:54:13.200
But if you put in an
online survey like this,

00:54:13.200 --> 00:54:16.390
someone's just going to take the
whole thing, dump it into GPT4,

00:54:16.390 --> 00:54:18.510
and then just copy the
answers straight back

00:54:18.510 --> 00:54:21.090
onto your pairwise responses.

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:23.880
We've had several studies in
the past where an annotator had

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:27.290
95-plus-percent
agreement with GPT4,

00:54:27.290 --> 00:54:31.150
and you have to wonder
what's going on there.

00:54:31.150 --> 00:54:35.650
And so despite the fact that
pairwise feedback is easier

00:54:35.650 --> 00:54:39.430
to collect than
supervised imitation data,

00:54:39.430 --> 00:54:41.540
there are still
significant issues.

00:54:41.540 --> 00:54:45.310
And I'd be remiss to not point
out, the many things that

00:54:45.310 --> 00:54:49.060
have been written about
the kinds of problems

00:54:49.060 --> 00:54:51.130
that this creates if you
try to outsource this

00:54:51.130 --> 00:54:53.110
to third world countries.

00:54:53.110 --> 00:54:56.320
There's pricing concerns
and lots of ethical issues

00:54:56.320 --> 00:54:58.690
that you all should
be aware of if you're

00:54:58.690 --> 00:55:02.050
going to be, in the future, be
a part of these kinds of data

00:55:02.050 --> 00:55:03.160
collection pipelines.

00:55:03.160 --> 00:55:05.110
You want to make sure
to get high quality data

00:55:05.110 --> 00:55:07.120
and to also make
sure that people

00:55:07.120 --> 00:55:09.820
are being paid living wage.

00:55:09.820 --> 00:55:14.020
The other thing to be aware of,
this is in the bias and safety

00:55:14.020 --> 00:55:15.850
angle, because for
alignment, I think this

00:55:15.850 --> 00:55:18.670
is a really important
thing to also touch on.

00:55:18.670 --> 00:55:21.580
Is that in some sense,
RLHF and alignment

00:55:21.580 --> 00:55:23.028
come at the end of the pipeline.

00:55:23.028 --> 00:55:25.070
And because they come at
the end of the pipeline,

00:55:25.070 --> 00:55:27.790
they have very strong
influence on model behaviors.

00:55:27.790 --> 00:55:30.590
One of the papers that
actually Percy and my postdoc,

00:55:30.590 --> 00:55:36.070
[? Shivani, ?] and [? Essen, ?]
a postdoc on mine worked on was

00:55:36.070 --> 00:55:37.660
this paper on trying
to figure out,

00:55:37.660 --> 00:55:41.350
how do subjective opinions of
LMs align with different groups

00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:42.050
of people?

00:55:42.050 --> 00:55:43.930
And one of the really
interesting patterns

00:55:43.930 --> 00:55:46.300
that we found was
actually for InstructGPT,

00:55:46.300 --> 00:55:50.770
these are old models but
now, still useful models.

00:55:50.770 --> 00:55:54.280
These models somehow became more
aligned with Southeast Asian

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:56.000
religions than before.

00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:58.420
And then, we looked in the
appendix of InstructGPT

00:55:58.420 --> 00:56:00.820
and actually, what's the
nationality of the people doing

00:56:00.820 --> 00:56:01.550
the annotation?

00:56:01.550 --> 00:56:04.713
It's Filipino, and
Bangladeshi, and 17% American.

00:56:04.713 --> 00:56:06.380
I was like, oh, it's
kind of surprising,

00:56:06.380 --> 00:56:08.950
but does circumstantial
evidence lines up

00:56:08.950 --> 00:56:09.950
with this kind of thing.

00:56:09.950 --> 00:56:11.533
And so you do have
to be very careful,

00:56:11.533 --> 00:56:13.870
because this is the thing
that in some sense goes out

00:56:13.870 --> 00:56:16.111
and ships.

00:56:16.111 --> 00:56:20.290
Others have also
noted that depending

00:56:20.290 --> 00:56:23.530
on the annotator, what they pay
attention to is very different.

00:56:23.530 --> 00:56:27.920
I really like this paper from
Husking, Blunsom, and Bartolo,

00:56:27.920 --> 00:56:31.010
where they basically study two
different kinds of annotators.

00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:32.560
One is the authors,
and they're very

00:56:32.560 --> 00:56:34.960
motivated to judge
things correctly

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:36.820
with quotes and
then Crowdworkers.

00:56:36.820 --> 00:56:39.130
And what they really
find is crowdworkers

00:56:39.130 --> 00:56:41.860
don't pay much
attention to factuality.

00:56:41.860 --> 00:56:43.480
That's this row here.

00:56:43.480 --> 00:56:45.460
They pay more attention
to formatting.

00:56:45.460 --> 00:56:47.290
So depending on the
annotator, you're

00:56:47.290 --> 00:56:49.160
getting different
kinds of feedback,

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:52.262
even though you're asking
them the same things.

00:56:52.262 --> 00:56:55.900
And so increasingly,
people have turned

00:56:55.900 --> 00:56:58.720
to as with the
instruction tuning phase

00:56:58.720 --> 00:57:02.630
on AI feedback, where
LM generated feedback.

00:57:02.630 --> 00:57:06.670
And so there's been many works,
including some of our own,

00:57:06.670 --> 00:57:08.980
that have shown things
like, oh, if you

00:57:08.980 --> 00:57:11.420
try to get pairwise
feedback from GPT4,

00:57:11.420 --> 00:57:13.995
it has very strong
agreement from GPT4.

00:57:13.995 --> 00:57:17.410
[INAUDIBLE] estimated win
rates of models or responses

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:21.170
and the human estimated ones
on the y-axis, same here.

00:57:21.170 --> 00:57:23.220
The agreement between
human to human,

00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:25.790
which is the blue box
here, is roughly the same

00:57:25.790 --> 00:57:29.930
as the agreement between GPT4
and human but is much cheaper.

00:57:29.930 --> 00:57:34.070
So there's lots of
reasons, I think why

00:57:34.070 --> 00:57:37.040
AI feedback has become popular.

00:57:37.040 --> 00:57:40.760
And it has been used
very extensively in RLHF.

00:57:40.760 --> 00:57:42.470
So if you look at
ultra feedback, which

00:57:42.470 --> 00:57:45.320
I think is one of the very
popular open source data

00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:48.080
sets for off policy RLHF.

00:57:48.080 --> 00:57:52.880
You see this if Zephyr-7b I
think was a Hugging Face Effort,

00:57:52.880 --> 00:57:56.610
I want to say last year, to
build a big, strong, open model.

00:57:56.610 --> 00:57:58.110
And the reason why
I bring this up--

00:57:58.110 --> 00:58:01.020
I think Zephyr is probably not
the most well known model--

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:05.780
but one of the things I remember
about the Hugging Face model

00:58:05.780 --> 00:58:08.720
process was initially,
they were really interested

00:58:08.720 --> 00:58:10.040
in human data collection.

00:58:10.040 --> 00:58:13.310
They were convinced that
human crowdworker, if you

00:58:13.310 --> 00:58:15.620
paid them enough and
got the right vendors,

00:58:15.620 --> 00:58:19.310
would outperform AI
generated feedback.

00:58:19.310 --> 00:58:21.860
But late in the
process, they realized

00:58:21.860 --> 00:58:24.440
GPT4 generated feedback
for this kind of thing

00:58:24.440 --> 00:58:25.680
just worked much better.

00:58:25.680 --> 00:58:27.800
And so a more modern
example of this

00:58:27.800 --> 00:58:33.110
is Tulu3, which is a
post-training paper/project out

00:58:33.110 --> 00:58:34.310
of AI two.

00:58:34.310 --> 00:58:36.030
And they've done roughly this.

00:58:36.030 --> 00:58:37.610
They take different
prompts, they

00:58:37.610 --> 00:58:40.260
have lots of different
models to generate responses,

00:58:40.260 --> 00:58:46.340
and they have an LM rate these
to get chosen versus non-chosen.

00:58:46.340 --> 00:58:48.620
And this really
all just goes back

00:58:48.620 --> 00:58:51.680
to the classic paper would be--

00:58:51.680 --> 00:58:54.030
the anthropic paper
on constitutional AI,

00:58:54.030 --> 00:58:58.520
which I think really
planted a flag on the ground

00:58:58.520 --> 00:59:01.310
in terms of AI
feedback being used

00:59:01.310 --> 00:59:03.183
for this kind of
alignment process.

00:59:05.690 --> 00:59:08.360
Finally, the last thing I
want to talk about for data

00:59:08.360 --> 00:59:10.340
is length effects.

00:59:10.340 --> 00:59:14.780
I think when we did the
annotation, one of the things

00:59:14.780 --> 00:59:17.388
that we saw was I think many
of you saw the longer response

00:59:17.388 --> 00:59:19.680
and you're like, this is more
detailed, I like details,

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:20.630
this is good.

00:59:20.630 --> 00:59:25.940
It's not just you, models and
people all have this bias.

00:59:25.940 --> 00:59:29.240
And so people have found that
models that people have thought

00:59:29.240 --> 00:59:32.880
were better were, in fact,
maybe only just also longer.

00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:34.790
And then AI feedback
seems to make models

00:59:34.790 --> 00:59:36.500
just generally longer.

00:59:36.500 --> 00:59:38.000
And so this is
always a confounder

00:59:38.000 --> 00:59:40.280
that you want to be
careful of length

00:59:40.280 --> 00:59:43.740
as a confounder for
general preference OK,

00:59:43.740 --> 00:59:45.140
there was a question there.

00:59:45.140 --> 00:59:48.590
Please expand on off policy
and on policy [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:48.590 --> 00:59:52.840
OK, yeah, I'll talk about
off versus on policy later.

00:59:52.840 --> 00:59:55.940
Off policy, I think
I mentioned it here,

00:59:55.940 --> 00:59:59.540
is going to refer to you
collect these pairwise feedback

00:59:59.540 --> 01:00:01.550
things separately.

01:00:01.550 --> 01:00:03.862
They're not collected from
the outputs of your model.

01:00:03.862 --> 01:00:05.070
Maybe your model is involved.

01:00:05.070 --> 01:00:07.370
For example in Tulu,
you've got all these kind

01:00:07.370 --> 01:00:08.940
of off policy data on the left.

01:00:08.940 --> 01:00:10.440
That's models that
are not your own.

01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:13.080
But you also have on
policy data from yourself.

01:00:13.080 --> 01:00:16.530
So the off policy data tells you
about the landscape of places

01:00:16.530 --> 01:00:18.690
you're not at,
and on policy data

01:00:18.690 --> 01:00:20.603
tells you how to
refine yourself.

01:00:20.603 --> 01:00:21.103
Yes.

01:00:24.330 --> 01:00:32.820
The way this human aspect works
were sampling prompts and asking

01:00:32.820 --> 01:00:35.550
humans to rate them.

01:00:35.550 --> 01:00:39.540
Have people ever,
instead of sampling

01:00:39.540 --> 01:00:43.180
prompts that you don't have the
answer to and then ask them,

01:00:43.180 --> 01:00:49.300
do you have existing in the
world from any number of places,

01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:52.020
there are existing prompts
that we know the answers to

01:00:52.020 --> 01:00:53.350
and we have that data saved?

01:00:53.350 --> 01:00:56.460
We don't need to do anything
to receive that data.

01:00:56.460 --> 01:01:02.400
Have people done alignment
tuning using that style of data

01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:06.550
instead of asking humans or LMs?

01:01:06.550 --> 01:01:07.210
Yes, OK.

01:01:07.210 --> 01:01:08.470
That's a good question.

01:01:08.470 --> 01:01:12.300
And it's like, why don't we
do RLHF on-- or can we do RLHF

01:01:12.300 --> 01:01:16.380
on domains where the answer is
one way of putting the question.

01:01:16.380 --> 01:01:20.340
And part of the answer is
the next lecture on Thursday

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:22.270
is that where do we
really the answer?

01:01:22.270 --> 01:01:22.770
Math.

01:01:22.770 --> 01:01:24.490
The answer very well for math.

01:01:24.490 --> 01:01:28.650
And we can do exactly RL against
math, and it works very well.

01:01:28.650 --> 01:01:31.140
People also do
things like, here's

01:01:31.140 --> 01:01:32.760
a long form response
from an expert

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:34.180
that has already been written.

01:01:34.180 --> 01:01:36.600
Now, can you judge
it given this?

01:01:36.600 --> 01:01:37.450
That helps.

01:01:37.450 --> 01:01:40.260
But one thing that I think
is important to keep in mind

01:01:40.260 --> 01:01:42.430
is there's for a lot of
these open ended tasks,

01:01:42.430 --> 01:01:44.100
there's many correct
answers, and it's

01:01:44.100 --> 01:01:45.850
very difficult to judge
which are correct.

01:01:45.850 --> 01:01:48.040
If I have a new fact
in the LM response,

01:01:48.040 --> 01:01:49.365
is that a correct fact or not?

01:01:49.365 --> 01:01:51.900
It doesn't really solve
those problems, yes.

01:01:51.900 --> 01:01:53.070
Follow up question.

01:01:53.070 --> 01:01:57.270
So thinking about, for instance,
[? the anthropic ?] referenced.

01:01:57.270 --> 01:02:01.210
There's this relatively specific
and small constitutional thing.

01:02:01.210 --> 01:02:03.018
But we can even--

01:02:03.018 --> 01:02:06.510
same thing with [? values ?]
to make this work for open

01:02:06.510 --> 01:02:12.750
ended things, because we have
any number of sources of,

01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:15.690
here's a problem, here are
values that we as a society

01:02:15.690 --> 01:02:17.550
think are relevant,
whether that's

01:02:17.550 --> 01:02:24.510
surveys or politics or articles,
just any number of sources.

01:02:24.510 --> 01:02:28.030
You got another anthropic
[? team ?] like let people vote.

01:02:28.030 --> 01:02:31.140
But that seems
small in comparison.

01:02:31.140 --> 01:02:33.720
Yeah, I guess there's lots
of different things being

01:02:33.720 --> 01:02:34.810
mixed in on that comment.

01:02:34.810 --> 01:02:37.650
You could do deliberative
democracy style stuff

01:02:37.650 --> 01:02:38.440
to align models.

01:02:38.440 --> 01:02:39.580
That is certainly a thing.

01:02:39.580 --> 01:02:41.413
There's also, I
guess, the thing that

01:02:41.413 --> 01:02:43.080
feels close to what
you're talking about

01:02:43.080 --> 01:02:46.360
is almost giving the
annotator sources almost,

01:02:46.360 --> 01:02:47.430
that are relevant.

01:02:47.430 --> 01:02:48.850
And I'm sure those things help.

01:02:48.850 --> 01:02:51.090
There have been works
on showing people

01:02:51.090 --> 01:02:53.710
expert written responses when
they do the pairwise judgment,

01:02:53.710 --> 01:02:55.170
and so on.

01:02:55.170 --> 01:02:57.280
But I think it's
not a silver bullet.

01:02:57.280 --> 01:02:59.280
All of this kind
of UI interventions

01:02:59.280 --> 01:03:01.470
definitely help, but
it's not necessarily

01:03:01.470 --> 01:03:04.530
going to in one stroke,
solve the problem.

01:03:04.530 --> 01:03:05.906
OK, yes.

01:03:05.906 --> 01:03:07.650
[INAUDIBLE] question.

01:03:07.650 --> 01:03:11.770
[INAUDIBLE] paper
where you have I

01:03:11.770 --> 01:03:16.600
want to ask you your
other [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:16.600 --> 01:03:21.160
So there's a bias from
deep, deep [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:21.160 --> 01:03:22.480
generation [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:22.480 --> 01:03:26.860
Yes, there's a very
strong or very detectable

01:03:26.860 --> 01:03:30.140
self-preference for most
models for their own outputs.

01:03:30.140 --> 01:03:32.270
And so when you
use this for evals,

01:03:32.270 --> 01:03:34.900
which many people,
including I have done,

01:03:34.900 --> 01:03:38.570
you have to be very careful
for that self bias, absolutely.

01:03:38.570 --> 01:03:39.800
Then there's lots of hands.

01:03:39.800 --> 01:03:41.582
But yes, I think you were first.

01:03:41.582 --> 01:03:43.082
Do you think there
must be balancing

01:03:43.082 --> 01:03:45.165
the amount of information
you get just from having

01:03:45.165 --> 01:03:46.460
a model for your own outputs?

01:03:46.460 --> 01:03:49.460
And then, I guess using
models to do its own feedback.

01:03:49.460 --> 01:03:53.270
So a heuristic for how many
iterations that you can do.

01:03:53.270 --> 01:03:55.780
And does that change if
you say like a sampling

01:03:55.780 --> 01:03:57.850
of different models [INAUDIBLE]?

01:03:57.850 --> 01:03:59.560
Yeah, I guess the
question of how much

01:03:59.560 --> 01:04:02.050
can you extract out of models
doing things to themselves

01:04:02.050 --> 01:04:03.410
is an interesting question?

01:04:03.410 --> 01:04:07.400
But I guess in some ways, the
information theoretic bound,

01:04:07.400 --> 01:04:10.550
so to speak, is very, very high,
because technically the model

01:04:10.550 --> 01:04:13.040
ingests the entire
pre-training corpus

01:04:13.040 --> 01:04:15.222
and that could be stored
somewhere in the model.

01:04:15.222 --> 01:04:16.680
And depending on
how you prompt it,

01:04:16.680 --> 01:04:18.300
you might get an
amazing model out.

01:04:18.300 --> 01:04:21.320
And so it's possible
that based on how you're

01:04:21.320 --> 01:04:24.120
using the model as part of
your self refinement loop,

01:04:24.120 --> 01:04:26.490
you can extract more
capabilities out of the model.

01:04:26.490 --> 01:04:28.250
And we don't what the
upper bound of that

01:04:28.250 --> 01:04:33.080
is because basically,
the inputs are just vast.

01:04:33.080 --> 01:04:34.820
But I do think
there's practically

01:04:34.820 --> 01:04:38.000
lots of papers that have studied
this question, how much can

01:04:38.000 --> 01:04:39.072
self-improvement help?

01:04:39.072 --> 01:04:41.280
What's the scaling properties
and so on and so forth?

01:04:41.280 --> 01:04:45.260
I think ultimately, it's
a very empirical question.

01:04:45.260 --> 01:04:47.660
OK, yes.

01:04:47.660 --> 01:04:56.640
[INAUDIBLE] because
compared to SRT,

01:04:56.640 --> 01:05:00.320
so basically SRT
is the best answer

01:05:00.320 --> 01:05:01.910
or we have a label for that.

01:05:01.910 --> 01:05:05.360
But we assume that
we don't have label,

01:05:05.360 --> 01:05:09.050
but we have that reward models
to give it like a preference

01:05:09.050 --> 01:05:12.140
so that it's [INAUDIBLE] thing.

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:14.640
Yeah, so maybe I should just
go through the next slides

01:05:14.640 --> 01:05:16.640
because actually, that
will actually explain it.

01:05:16.640 --> 01:05:20.590
So not only does it help us
get through the rest of this

01:05:20.590 --> 01:05:23.110
hopefully quickly,
but also I think

01:05:23.110 --> 01:05:24.860
it'll explain your question.

01:05:24.860 --> 01:05:27.730
So OK, so now let's
talk about methods.

01:05:27.730 --> 01:05:29.390
Our goal is to do this thing.

01:05:29.390 --> 01:05:32.030
I want to find a policy
to maximize my rewards.

01:05:32.030 --> 01:05:34.190
So I need to tell you
what the rewards are.

01:05:34.190 --> 01:05:36.530
And I need to tell you what
the maximization process is.

01:05:36.530 --> 01:05:38.620
So let's do that now.

01:05:38.620 --> 01:05:40.470
I think, as you
can probably tell

01:05:40.470 --> 01:05:42.970
by the frequency with which I'm
referring to the InstructGPT

01:05:42.970 --> 01:05:46.270
paper, the whole subarea
of instruction tuning

01:05:46.270 --> 01:05:50.050
and post-training is very
closely tied to InstructGPT.

01:05:50.050 --> 01:05:51.940
So from the
InstructGPT paper, you

01:05:51.940 --> 01:05:55.960
have equation 2, which is
this, objective that describes

01:05:55.960 --> 01:05:57.200
what we're optimizing.

01:05:57.200 --> 01:05:59.870
So we've got this
r theta of x of y.

01:05:59.870 --> 01:06:00.925
This is our reward.

01:06:00.925 --> 01:06:02.300
And I'll define
that in a moment.

01:06:02.300 --> 01:06:04.780
And then we've got
this second terms,

01:06:04.780 --> 01:06:09.830
the log ratio of my RL policy
divided by my SFT output.

01:06:09.830 --> 01:06:11.340
So what is this object?

01:06:11.340 --> 01:06:14.540
This is the KL divergence
between my RL policy

01:06:14.540 --> 01:06:16.500
and my original SFT model.

01:06:16.500 --> 01:06:19.280
So it's saying when I do
RL, don't move too far

01:06:19.280 --> 01:06:20.340
from where I started.

01:06:20.340 --> 01:06:23.340
And then this second
line, this gamma term,

01:06:23.340 --> 01:06:26.180
this is basically saying
keep doing pre-training

01:06:26.180 --> 01:06:28.370
while you do RL so you
don't catastrophically

01:06:28.370 --> 01:06:30.630
forget if you keep doing this.

01:06:30.630 --> 01:06:32.310
Lots of people don't
do the second step.

01:06:32.310 --> 01:06:35.730
But this KL thing
it's a standard thing.

01:06:35.730 --> 01:06:37.400
It remains even today.

01:06:37.400 --> 01:06:39.570
So now, what is the reward?

01:06:39.570 --> 01:06:44.300
The reward is this
thing at the very top.

01:06:44.300 --> 01:06:45.870
And so maybe that's
small equation.

01:06:45.870 --> 01:06:47.460
So I'll talk through
what that is.

01:06:47.460 --> 01:06:52.500
So there is a hypothesized
model of the world that exists.

01:06:52.500 --> 01:06:55.010
So what is the hypothesized
model of the world?

01:06:55.010 --> 01:06:59.030
It's that every single output
in the world, that a language

01:06:59.030 --> 01:07:00.860
model could output
every single sequence

01:07:00.860 --> 01:07:05.010
has a scalar value,
r, associated with it.

01:07:05.010 --> 01:07:06.900
And we don't observe
what that r is.

01:07:06.900 --> 01:07:09.290
And when a person
rates it, when they

01:07:09.290 --> 01:07:13.280
do a pairwise rating of A
versus B, what they do is they

01:07:13.280 --> 01:07:16.230
compare the two rewards
of those two sequences.

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:18.620
And based on the difference,
they'll take a coin flip.

01:07:18.620 --> 01:07:21.860
So this is a logistic model
of the difference of the two

01:07:21.860 --> 01:07:22.410
rewards.

01:07:22.410 --> 01:07:23.810
So every sequence has a reward.

01:07:23.810 --> 01:07:26.060
When I do pairwise comparisons,
I take the difference,

01:07:26.060 --> 01:07:27.410
and I flip a coin right.

01:07:27.410 --> 01:07:30.292
This is the Bradley-Terry
model of human preferences.

01:07:30.292 --> 01:07:31.500
And this is what's happening.

01:07:31.500 --> 01:07:33.950
And so when we want to
optimize the reward, what we're

01:07:33.950 --> 01:07:37.400
trying to do is we want to
output the sequence that

01:07:37.400 --> 01:07:38.910
has the highest r.

01:07:38.910 --> 01:07:40.500
And r is not
something we observe.

01:07:40.500 --> 01:07:44.730
We only observe noisy pairwise
comparisons through r theta.

01:07:44.730 --> 01:07:46.790
So that's what we do.

01:07:46.790 --> 01:07:48.600
So that's the objective.

01:07:48.600 --> 01:07:50.720
So that's what we're
trying to optimize.

01:07:50.720 --> 01:07:53.480
And so now, let's
talk about how.

01:07:53.480 --> 01:07:56.120
And to be clear, I'm only
going to talk about PPO,

01:07:56.120 --> 01:07:58.140
which is the OG algorithm.

01:07:58.140 --> 01:07:59.760
This is what appears
in InstructGPT

01:07:59.760 --> 01:08:01.130
and a lot of the OpenAI stuff.

01:08:01.130 --> 01:08:03.180
I'm going to talk about
it only very briefly,

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:05.680
and then I'll talk about it in
much more detail on Thursday,

01:08:05.680 --> 01:08:07.720
because it more
naturally belongs there.

01:08:07.720 --> 01:08:11.640
We're going to do a lot more
real RL on that lecture.

01:08:11.640 --> 01:08:15.570
So at a conceptual level,
remember what we want to do

01:08:15.570 --> 01:08:19.060
is we want to optimize
the reward of some policy.

01:08:19.060 --> 01:08:20.590
That's the left term here.

01:08:20.590 --> 01:08:22.899
And what's a good way
of optimizing something?

01:08:22.899 --> 01:08:24.370
Well, let's take some gradients.

01:08:24.370 --> 01:08:25.600
Let's take gradient descent.

01:08:25.600 --> 01:08:28.600
So that's the very top
left equation here.

01:08:28.600 --> 01:08:32.138
Now, we can do a
little bit of math.

01:08:32.138 --> 01:08:33.930
And if we take the
gradient of this object,

01:08:33.930 --> 01:08:37.649
we can write down that this is
equivalent to the expectation

01:08:37.649 --> 01:08:42.569
of the reward multiplied
by the gradient of p theta.

01:08:42.569 --> 01:08:44.500
And so this is very natural.

01:08:44.500 --> 01:08:46.680
What you're doing is you
take your normal gradients

01:08:46.680 --> 01:08:49.390
that you normally take in doing
like pre-training or whatever.

01:08:49.390 --> 01:08:51.069
This is saying p theta of z.

01:08:51.069 --> 01:08:54.210
I want to maximize that
probability and I maximize or I

01:08:54.210 --> 01:08:55.450
multiply that with r.

01:08:55.450 --> 01:08:57.569
So if my rewards
are positive, I want

01:08:57.569 --> 01:08:59.050
to upweight those probabilities.

01:08:59.050 --> 01:09:00.720
If my rewards are
negative, I want

01:09:00.720 --> 01:09:02.279
to downweight those
probabilities.

01:09:02.279 --> 01:09:04.060
This is the policy
gradient theorem.

01:09:04.060 --> 01:09:05.163
This is reinforce.

01:09:05.163 --> 01:09:07.080
If you've taken RL class
or something like it,

01:09:07.080 --> 01:09:09.000
you've definitely
seen this before.

01:09:09.000 --> 01:09:10.489
Now, what is PPO?

01:09:10.489 --> 01:09:16.630
A PPO, I think, is normally
a very intimidating object,

01:09:16.630 --> 01:09:18.790
but I think it is
actually quite simple.

01:09:18.790 --> 01:09:20.560
So there's two
steps that happen.

01:09:20.560 --> 01:09:22.960
First, instead of
taking a reward,

01:09:22.960 --> 01:09:24.729
we look at what's
called an advantage.

01:09:24.729 --> 01:09:30.569
An advantage just to gloss over
a lot of the details involved,

01:09:30.569 --> 01:09:33.960
an advantage is basically
a variance reduced version

01:09:33.960 --> 01:09:35.189
of the reward.

01:09:35.189 --> 01:09:37.020
If you go through
the math, you can

01:09:37.020 --> 01:09:39.149
notice that I can
subtract any constant.

01:09:39.149 --> 01:09:41.670
Or in fact, I can
subtract any state

01:09:41.670 --> 01:09:44.430
dependent variable from
r and this gradient

01:09:44.430 --> 01:09:45.760
will still be correct.

01:09:45.760 --> 01:09:49.530
That means that I can rewrite
this reward potentially

01:09:49.530 --> 01:09:52.092
after subtracting any
baseline values that I want.

01:09:52.092 --> 01:09:53.800
And let's say we call
that the advantage.

01:09:53.800 --> 01:09:57.510
Now, not only that, maybe I want
to take multiple gradient steps

01:09:57.510 --> 01:10:01.800
after sampling from p theta
once, what's called essentially,

01:10:01.800 --> 01:10:04.840
sampling from one rollout
and going almost off policy.

01:10:04.840 --> 01:10:07.350
To enable that, I have
to essentially have

01:10:07.350 --> 01:10:08.860
importance weighting
corrections,

01:10:08.860 --> 01:10:12.030
because the more steps
I take, the more stale

01:10:12.030 --> 01:10:14.290
my original samples become.

01:10:14.290 --> 01:10:16.030
And so this is
what's called TRPO.

01:10:16.030 --> 01:10:18.510
You basically make corrections
for all the gradient

01:10:18.510 --> 01:10:19.390
steps you take.

01:10:19.390 --> 01:10:22.860
And then, you constrain
yourself to stay close.

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:25.300
Now, PPO takes the
final step and says,

01:10:25.300 --> 01:10:28.080
instead of explicitly
constraining myself

01:10:28.080 --> 01:10:31.150
to stay close to my old policy
using this KL constraint,

01:10:31.150 --> 01:10:35.310
maybe what I can do is I can
just clip the probability

01:10:35.310 --> 01:10:38.250
ratios, and this will
naturally incentivize the model

01:10:38.250 --> 01:10:40.510
to stay close to
the original policy.

01:10:40.510 --> 01:10:43.020
So this is PPO in one slide.

01:10:43.020 --> 01:10:45.090
I'm not going to go into
this with too much more

01:10:45.090 --> 01:10:48.000
detail, because actually, this
won't be the primary algorithm

01:10:48.000 --> 01:10:51.720
that I want to just go through
the rest of the lecture with.

01:10:51.720 --> 01:10:57.018
So at least in the open research
space and the academic space,

01:10:57.018 --> 01:10:59.560
a lot of the question that I
think people were concerned with

01:10:59.560 --> 01:11:01.640
was, can we get rid of PPO?

01:11:01.640 --> 01:11:04.240
We will see this theme
on Thursday as well.

01:11:04.240 --> 01:11:06.460
PPO is very complicated.

01:11:06.460 --> 01:11:10.120
And we have debated but decided
against having you implement PPO

01:11:10.120 --> 01:11:12.160
because it will be suffering.

01:11:12.160 --> 01:11:15.250
And so lots of people thought,
can we get rid of PPO?

01:11:15.250 --> 01:11:17.420
And they tried other
reasonable things.

01:11:17.420 --> 01:11:20.830
And I will explain those
reasonable things like maybe we

01:11:20.830 --> 01:11:22.370
can SFT on the pairs.

01:11:22.370 --> 01:11:25.360
But for each of the pairs,
we can prepend a good token

01:11:25.360 --> 01:11:31.340
for the chosen good outputs and
bad token to the bad outputs.

01:11:31.340 --> 01:11:34.130
And then I can just condition
on good when I generate.

01:11:34.130 --> 01:11:35.390
That does not work very well.

01:11:35.390 --> 01:11:37.760
I can train the model only
on the preferred output.

01:11:37.760 --> 01:11:40.750
That also does not
work super well.

01:11:40.750 --> 01:11:44.770
I can use a reward model, sample
the best one out of those,

01:11:44.770 --> 01:11:46.040
and then train on those.

01:11:46.040 --> 01:11:48.700
That works OK, but
maybe not that great.

01:11:48.700 --> 01:11:50.780
And so people tried
all these variants.

01:11:50.780 --> 01:11:54.230
But what really stuck
was basically DPO.

01:11:54.230 --> 01:11:57.820
And I think the reason why it
caught on as much as it did

01:11:57.820 --> 01:12:01.690
was because they removed a
lot of the complexity of PPO

01:12:01.690 --> 01:12:04.780
and worked relatively well.

01:12:04.780 --> 01:12:08.630
And so you get rid of the
reward model that exists in PPO.

01:12:08.630 --> 01:12:10.540
This is used to
calculate the advantage.

01:12:10.540 --> 01:12:13.148
We get rid of any of the arm
policy stuff, the importance

01:12:13.148 --> 01:12:14.690
ratio thing that I
was talking about.

01:12:14.690 --> 01:12:16.065
You just get rid
of all of those.

01:12:16.065 --> 01:12:18.230
Instead we go back
to the basics.

01:12:18.230 --> 01:12:21.410
We take gradient steps on
the log loss of good things,

01:12:21.410 --> 01:12:23.350
and then we take
negative gradient steps

01:12:23.350 --> 01:12:25.430
on log losses of bad stuff.

01:12:25.430 --> 01:12:28.750
We go back to very
simple basic things.

01:12:28.750 --> 01:12:31.360
And the last part of what
I want to talk about today

01:12:31.360 --> 01:12:34.010
is just deriving
the DPO formula.

01:12:34.010 --> 01:12:35.570
So what is our goal?

01:12:35.570 --> 01:12:38.390
Our goal is to optimize this
quantity at the very top.

01:12:38.390 --> 01:12:41.720
This is just a rewriting of
that InstructGPT equation.

01:12:41.720 --> 01:12:43.300
I have a reward
at the very front,

01:12:43.300 --> 01:12:44.950
and then I have a KL divergence.

01:12:44.950 --> 01:12:48.740
Like this keeps me-- this is
pi theta close to my reference.

01:12:48.740 --> 01:12:50.620
So this is very natural.

01:12:50.620 --> 01:12:52.700
So the first thing
I'm going to do

01:12:52.700 --> 01:12:56.040
is I'm going to assume
that my policy, pi theta,

01:12:56.040 --> 01:12:57.510
is not actually
a neural network.

01:12:57.510 --> 01:12:59.968
I'm going to assume it's an
arbitrary function of any kind.

01:12:59.968 --> 01:13:04.280
And if I do that, then I
can write down essentially

01:13:04.280 --> 01:13:06.870
what the optimal
policy looks like.

01:13:06.870 --> 01:13:08.370
It has this form.

01:13:08.370 --> 01:13:10.430
It's the exponential
of the reward

01:13:10.430 --> 01:13:13.700
and it's multiplying
pi ref, the reference

01:13:13.700 --> 01:13:15.380
distribution over here.

01:13:15.380 --> 01:13:18.960
And we can solve for the implied
reward by solving for our xy.

01:13:18.960 --> 01:13:20.960
And the clever part
about DPO is now

01:13:20.960 --> 01:13:25.310
to say, OK, it basically means
that every policy, instead

01:13:25.310 --> 01:13:27.800
of thinking about policies,
I can think about rewards

01:13:27.800 --> 01:13:29.390
because the two are
one and the same

01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:32.750
under this non-parametric
assumption.

01:13:32.750 --> 01:13:34.250
And so what you
do is remember, I

01:13:34.250 --> 01:13:36.210
have these two
pieces, the left side,

01:13:36.210 --> 01:13:40.100
this is the Bradley-Terry
equation from the Stiennon

01:13:40.100 --> 01:13:42.030
paper, the one
before InstructGPT.

01:13:42.030 --> 01:13:45.650
And on the right side,
this is the DPO equivalence

01:13:45.650 --> 01:13:46.440
I wrote down.

01:13:46.440 --> 01:13:51.030
And then now, what I can do is
I can plug in these rewards, r,

01:13:51.030 --> 01:13:54.630
into this objective, and
I can minimize this loss.

01:13:54.630 --> 01:13:58.910
I can say, what I want to do is
now I want to find a policy such

01:13:58.910 --> 01:14:01.400
that the implied
reward for that policy

01:14:01.400 --> 01:14:03.890
has the highest
probability of generating

01:14:03.890 --> 01:14:05.100
my pairwise comparisons.

01:14:05.100 --> 01:14:06.950
So now, I've taken
an RL problem,

01:14:06.950 --> 01:14:09.800
and I have turned it
into a maximum likelihood

01:14:09.800 --> 01:14:14.600
problem, a problem that's
very much similar conceptually

01:14:14.600 --> 01:14:16.470
to something like pre-training.

01:14:16.470 --> 01:14:18.780
All we're doing is
maximizing the probabilities.

01:14:18.780 --> 01:14:20.322
Except what we're
doing here is we're

01:14:20.322 --> 01:14:22.890
maximizing the probabilities
of the pairwise comparisons.

01:14:22.890 --> 01:14:24.210
So those are the key steps.

01:14:24.210 --> 01:14:26.690
Start by making the
non-parametric assumptions.

01:14:26.690 --> 01:14:28.790
Parameterize the
reward via the policy.

01:14:28.790 --> 01:14:32.905
And then optimize it using
the supervised losses.

01:14:32.905 --> 01:14:34.280
I think we're a
few minutes over.

01:14:34.280 --> 01:14:35.400
So we'll stop here.

01:14:35.400 --> 01:14:37.025
I think this is a
good place because we

01:14:37.025 --> 01:14:38.870
got through the
derivation of DPO,

01:14:38.870 --> 01:14:40.790
and we'll get through
the rest of RLHF

01:14:40.790 --> 01:14:43.130
at the start of next lecture.

01:14:43.130 --> 01:14:46.450
Thanks, everyone, for asking
lots of good questions.
